# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Rate equations and order
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="Rate equations and order of reaction",
    title="Rate equations, order and the rate constant",
    slug="chu1-rate-equations-order",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Write and use a rate equation, state the order with respect to each reactant and overall, find the units of the rate constant, and recognise zero, first and second order from graphs.",
    objectives=[
        "Define rate of reaction and the terms rate equation, order, overall order and rate constant",
        "Use a rate equation to calculate a rate, a rate constant or the effect of changing a concentration",
        "Derive the units of k for any overall order",
        "Recognise zero, first and second order from concentration-time and rate-concentration graphs",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Rate of reaction", "Rate equation", "Order of reaction", "Overall order", "Rate constant k", "Units of k"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A baker in Douala adds yeast to dough. On a warm day the dough rises quickly; in a cool store it rises slowly. Chemists want to say *how* quickly, with a number. For a reaction in solution we use the **rate**: how fast the concentration of a reactant falls (or a product rises) as time passes."),
        T(r"At Lower Sixth you met rate of reaction and collision theory. In this lesson we go further. We write an equation that links the rate to the concentrations of the reactants, and we use it to predict and calculate."),
        H("Rate, rate equation and order"),
        D(r"**Rate of reaction** is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time. Its usual unit is $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$.", title="Rate of reaction",
          formula=r"\text{rate} = -\frac{\Delta[\text{reactant}]}{\Delta t} = +\frac{\Delta[\text{product}]}{\Delta t}",
          note="Square brackets mean concentration in mol per dm3. A reactant's concentration falls, so we put a minus sign to make the rate positive."),
        D(r"For a reaction $\mathrm{A} + \mathrm{B} \rightarrow$ products, experiments often show that", title="The rate equation",
          formula=r"\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^m[\mathrm{B}]^n",
          note="m and n are the orders with respect to A and B. The overall order is m + n. k is the rate constant."),
        T(r"The **order with respect to A** is the power of $[\mathrm{A}]$ in the rate equation. If doubling $[\mathrm{A}]$ makes no difference to the rate, the order is 0. If it doubles the rate, the order is 1. If it quadruples the rate, the order is 2. The **overall order** is the sum of all the orders. The **rate constant** $k$ is the number that turns concentrations into a rate. It does not depend on concentration. It changes with temperature, and a catalyst changes it."),
        MISTAKE("Writing the rate equation from the balanced equation", r"The orders come from experiment only. For $2\mathrm{NO} + 2\mathrm{H_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{N_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O}$ the measured rate equation is $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{H_2}]$. The balancing numbers are 2 and 2, but the orders are 2 and 1. Never copy the balancing numbers into the powers."),
        H("The units of k"),
        T(r"Put the units into the rate equation and solve for the units of $k$. The rate has units $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$ and each concentration has units $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, so the units of $k$ change with the overall order."),
        CODE("""Overall order   Rate equation example   Units of k
0               rate = k                mol dm-3 s-1
1               rate = k[A]             s-1
2               rate = k[A][B]          dm3 mol-1 s-1
3               rate = k[A]^2[B]        dm6 mol-2 s-1""", "text", title="Units of k by overall order"),
        EX("Worked example 1: units of k", intro=r"The rate equation for a reaction is $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{H_2}]$. Find the units of $k$.", steps=[
            S("Rearrange for k", math=r"k = \frac{\text{rate}}{[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{H_2}]}"),
            S("Insert units", math=r"k = \frac{\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}}{(\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}})^3}"),
            S("Simplify the powers of mol and dm", math=r"k = \mathrm{mol^{1-3}\,dm^{-3+9}\,s^{-1}} = \mathrm{mol^{-2}\,dm^{6}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("State the answer", text=r"The overall order is 3, and $k$ is in $\mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: calculating a rate and the effect of changing concentrations",
           intro=r"For a reaction, $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^2[\mathrm{B}]$ with $k = 0.50\ \mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$. (a) Find the rate when $[\mathrm{A}] = 0.20$ and $[\mathrm{B}] = 0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. (b) Find the new rate if $[\mathrm{A}]$ is doubled and $[\mathrm{B}]$ is halved.", steps=[
            S("(a) Substitute", math=r"\text{rate} = 0.50 \times (0.20)^2 \times 0.10 = 0.50 \times 0.040 \times 0.10"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"\text{rate} = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("(b) New concentrations", text=r"$[\mathrm{A}] = 0.40$ and $[\mathrm{B}] = 0.050\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$."),
            S("New rate", math=r"\text{rate} = 0.50 \times (0.40)^2 \times 0.050 = 4.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Compare using the orders", text=r"A is second order, so doubling gives $2^2 = 4$ times the rate. B is first order, so halving gives $\tfrac12$. Net factor $4 \times \tfrac12 = 2$, which agrees."),
        ], check=r"4.0\times10^{-3} \div 2.0\times10^{-3} = 2"),
        H("Reading the order from graphs"),
        T(r"Two kinds of graph tell you the order for one reactant. A **concentration-time** graph shows how $[\mathrm{A}]$ falls. The **rate** at any moment is minus the gradient of the tangent to the curve at that time. A **rate-concentration** graph plots the rate (found from tangents) against $[\mathrm{A}]$."),
        IMG("al-chu1-rate-graphs.svg", alt="Six small graphs. Concentration against time: zero order a straight falling line; first and second order curves that level off. Rate against concentration: zero order horizontal, first order a straight line through the origin, second order an upward curve.", caption="Figure 1. Zero, first and second order for a single reactant A."),
        KEY("Recognising order", "Use these shapes.", cards=[
            ["Zero order", r"[A]-time is a straight line. Rate-[A] is horizontal. Rate = k."],
            ["First order", r"[A]-time is a curve with a constant half-life. Rate-[A] is a straight line through the origin; its gradient is k."],
            ["Second order", r"Rate-[A] is a curve (rate proportional to [A] squared). A graph of rate against [A] squared is straight."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: rate from a tangent, then k", intro=r"The concentration of a reactant is $0.80\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ at $t = 0$. A tangent drawn to the curve at $t = 0$ passes through $(0\ \mathrm{s}, 0.80)$ and $(40\ \mathrm{s}, 0.00)$. The reaction is first order in this reactant. Find the initial rate and $k$.", steps=[
            S("Gradient of the tangent", math=r"\frac{0.00 - 0.80}{40 - 0} = -0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Rate is minus the gradient", math=r"\text{initial rate} = 0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("First order: rate = k[A]", math=r"k = \frac{0.020}{0.80} = 0.025\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using the average rate when the question asks for the rate at a time", r"The rate at a given time needs a tangent at that point. The change in concentration divided by the whole time gives only an average over that interval."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that k depends on temperature", r"Doubling a concentration changes the rate, not $k$. Raising the temperature changes $k$ (we study how in the Arrhenius lesson)."),
        QUIZ(r"A reaction has rate equation $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{P}][\mathrm{Q}]^2$. What are the units of $k$ if concentrations are in $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ and time in seconds?",
             [r"$\mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$", r"$\mathrm{s^{-1}}$", r"$\mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$", r"$\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$"], 2,
             r"The overall order is 3, so $k$ has units $\mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$.",
             feedback=[r"This is for overall order 2.", r"This is for overall order 1.", "", r"This is for overall order 0, where k has the same units as rate."], hint="Add the powers, then match the order to the table."),
        QUIZ(r"When $[\mathrm{A}]$ is doubled the rate becomes four times as large. What is the order with respect to A?",
             ["0", "1", "2", "4"], 2,
             r"$2^m = 4$ gives $m = 2$.",
             feedback=["Zero order would leave the rate unchanged.", "First order would double the rate.", "", r"The order is the power $m$, not the factor of 4."], hint=r"Solve $2^m = 4$."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The rate equation of a reaction is $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{X}][\mathrm{Y}]^2$. When $[\mathrm{X}] = 0.020$ and $[\mathrm{Y}] = 0.040\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ the rate is $4.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$. State the order with respect to each reactant and overall, and calculate $k$ with its units.",
           r"First order in X, second order in Y, overall order 3. $k = \dfrac{4.8\times10^{-5}}{0.020\times(0.040)^2} = \dfrac{4.8\times10^{-5}}{3.2\times10^{-5}} = 1.5\ \mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A student says: the equation $\mathrm{A} + 2\mathrm{B} \rightarrow \mathrm{C}$ means the rate equation is rate $= k[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{B}]^2$. Explain why this is not necessarily correct.",
           "The orders of a reaction can only be found by experiment, for example by measuring the rate while changing one concentration at a time. The balancing numbers show the amounts that react, not how many molecules take part in the slowest step, so the powers in the rate equation may be different."),
        PQ(r"For a reaction with rate $= k[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{B}]$, say what happens to the rate when (a) $[\mathrm{A}]$ is trebled, (b) $[\mathrm{A}]$ is trebled and $[\mathrm{B}]$ is doubled, (c) the temperature is raised.",
           r"(a) The rate is 3 times as large. (b) The rate is $3 \times 2 = 6$ times as large. (c) The rate rises because $k$ increases with temperature."),
        PQ("Describe how you would find the rate of a reaction at 60 s from a graph of concentration of a reactant against time, and say what shape the graph has if the reaction is zero order.",
           r"Draw a tangent to the curve at $t = 60$ s, find its gradient (change in concentration divided by change in time) and take the negative of the gradient, which is the rate. For a zero order reaction the graph is a straight line sloping downwards, because the rate is constant."),
        TIP("In the exam: rate equations, orders and units of k are tested in Paper 2 (structured questions with a table of data) and in Paper 1 (multiple choice). Always show the unit working for k."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Rate $= -\Delta[\text{reactant}]/\Delta t$, in $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$. On a concentration-time graph the rate at a time is minus the gradient of the tangent.",
            r"Rate equation: rate $= k[\mathrm{A}]^m[\mathrm{B}]^n$; the orders $m$, $n$ come from experiment, not from the balanced equation.",
            r"Overall order $= m + n$. The units of $k$ are $\mathrm{mol^{1-N}\,dm^{3N-3}\,s^{-1}}$ for overall order $N$.",
            "Zero order: straight line on [A]-time, horizontal on rate-[A]. First order: straight line through the origin on rate-[A].",
            r"$k$ is unchanged by concentration but changes with temperature and with a catalyst."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. Initial rates method
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="Initial rates and half-life",
    title="The initial rates method",
    slug="chu1-initial-rates-method",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Find the order with respect to each reactant from a table of initial rates, write the rate equation, calculate k with units, and predict rates for new concentrations.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain why initial rates are measured and how an experiment is designed to vary one concentration at a time",
        "Deduce orders from ratios of initial rates, including when two concentrations change together",
        "Write the rate equation and calculate k with its units",
        "Use a rate equation to predict the initial rate for new concentrations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Initial rate", "Order from ratios", "Rate equation", "Clock reaction", "Predicting a rate"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A water-treatment technician in Yaounde tests how fast a chemical removes colour from a sample. To learn how the speed depends on each chemical, she repeats the test many times, changing only one concentration each time. This is the idea behind the **initial rates method**."),
        H("Why measure the initial rate?"),
        T(r"As a reaction goes on, reactants are used up, products build up and the rate changes. At the very start, at $t = 0$, we know the concentrations exactly (we prepared them). Products have not had time to interfere. So we measure the **initial rate**, the rate at $t = 0$, in each experiment."),
        D(r"The **initial rates method** runs several experiments with different starting concentrations and measures the initial rate each time. By comparing experiments, we find how the rate depends on each concentration.", title="Initial rates method"),
        T(r"Two ways of finding an initial rate are common. One is to draw a tangent at $t = 0$ on a concentration-time graph. The other is a **clock reaction**: measure the time $t$ for a fixed small amount of product to form (for example, a colour change). The initial rate is then proportional to $1/t$."),
        H("Reading orders from a table"),
        KEY("The ratio rule", "Compare two experiments in which only one concentration changes.", cards=[
            ["Rate unchanged", r"The order is 0. That concentration does not appear in the rate equation."],
            ["Concentration x2, rate x2", "The order is 1."],
            ["Concentration x2, rate x4 (or x3, rate x9)", "The order is 2. In general, if the concentration changes by a factor f and the rate by a factor g, then f to the power m equals g."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: two reactants", intro=r"For $\mathrm{A} + \mathrm{B} \rightarrow$ products, the initial rate was measured at $25^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Find the rate equation, $k$ and the rate for $[\mathrm{A}] = 0.30$, $[\mathrm{B}] = 0.20$.", steps=[
            S("The data", text="Experiment 1: [A] = 0.10, [B] = 0.10, rate = 2.0e-4. Experiment 2: [A] = 0.20, [B] = 0.10, rate = 8.0e-4. Experiment 3: [A] = 0.10, [B] = 0.20, rate = 4.0e-4. (Concentrations in mol per dm3, rates in mol per dm3 per s.)"),
            S("Experiments 1 and 2: only A changes, doubled", math=r"\frac{8.0\times10^{-4}}{2.0\times10^{-4}} = 4 = 2^m \Rightarrow m = 2"),
            S("Experiments 1 and 3: only B changes, doubled", math=r"\frac{4.0\times10^{-4}}{2.0\times10^{-4}} = 2 = 2^n \Rightarrow n = 1"),
            S("Rate equation", math=r"\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^2[\mathrm{B}]"),
            S("k from experiment 1", math=r"k = \frac{2.0\times10^{-4}}{(0.10)^2(0.10)} = 0.20\ \mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}"),
            S("Predict the rate", math=r"\text{rate} = 0.20 \times (0.30)^2 \times 0.20 = 3.6\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"0.20\times(0.10)^2\times(0.20) = 4.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{(experiment 3 reproduced)}"),
        TIP(r"Always check your rate equation by substituting it into a different experiment from the one you used to find $k$."),
        H("When two concentrations change together"),
        T(r"Sometimes a table has been planned so that two concentrations change at once. Work in steps. Use the pairs where only one concentration changes first. Where none are available, divide the rate equations and solve for the unknown order."),
        EX("Worked example 2: three reactants", intro=r"For $\mathrm{P} + \mathrm{Q} + \mathrm{R} \rightarrow$ products, find the rate equation and $k$.", steps=[
            S("The data", text="Experiment 1: [P] = 0.10, [Q] = 0.10, [R] = 0.10, rate = 1.5e-3. Experiment 2: [P] = 0.10, [Q] = 0.20, [R] = 0.10, rate = 3.0e-3. Experiment 3: [P] = 0.10, [Q] = 0.20, [R] = 0.20, rate = 1.2e-2. Experiment 4: [P] = 0.20, [Q] = 0.10, [R] = 0.10, rate = 1.5e-3."),
            S("Experiments 1 and 2: only Q doubles; rate doubles", math=r"2^q = 2 \Rightarrow q = 1"),
            S("Experiments 2 and 3: only R doubles; rate becomes 4 times", math=r"\frac{1.2\times10^{-2}}{3.0\times10^{-3}} = 4 = 2^r \Rightarrow r = 2"),
            S("Experiments 1 and 4: only P doubles; rate unchanged", math=r"2^p = 1 \Rightarrow p = 0"),
            S("Rate equation", math=r"\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{Q}][\mathrm{R}]^2"),
            S("k from experiment 1", math=r"k = \frac{1.5\times10^{-3}}{(0.10)(0.10)^2} = 1.5\ \mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"1.5\times(0.20)(0.20)^2 = 1.2\times10^{-2}\ \text{(experiment 3 reproduced)}"),
        H("Clock reactions"),
        T(r"In the iodine clock reaction, peroxodisulfate ions oxidise iodide ions to iodine, $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + \mathrm{I_2}$. A little thiosulfate and starch are present, so a blue-black colour appears suddenly when a fixed amount of iodine has formed. The shorter the time, the faster the initial rate, so relative rate is proportional to $1/t$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: orders from clock times", intro=r"Three clock experiments (invented data) give these times for the same colour change. Find the order with respect to each ion.", steps=[
            S("The data", text="Experiment 1: [S2O8 2-] = 0.040, [I-] = 0.040, t = 100 s. Experiment 2: [S2O8 2-] = 0.080, [I-] = 0.040, t = 50 s. Experiment 3: [S2O8 2-] = 0.040, [I-] = 0.080, t = 50 s."),
            S("Relative rates as 1/t", math=r"\frac{1}{100} = 0.0100,\quad \frac{1}{50} = 0.0200,\quad \frac{1}{50} = 0.0200\ \ (\mathrm{s^{-1}})"),
            S("Experiments 1 and 2: peroxodisulfate doubled, rate doubled", text="First order in peroxodisulfate."),
            S("Experiments 1 and 3: iodide doubled, rate doubled", text="First order in iodide."),
            S("Rate equation", math=r"\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}}][\mathrm{I^-}]"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Comparing experiments where more than one concentration changes", r"If A and B both change between two experiments, the rate ratio mixes both effects. Choose pairs where only one concentration changes, or divide the rate equations."),
        MISTAKE("Using the time instead of 1/t", "In a clock reaction a longer time means a slower rate. Doubling the concentration of a first order reactant halves the time. Use 1/t before comparing."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to state the units of k", r"Work out the units from the rate equation. For $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^2[\mathrm{B}]$ they are $\mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$."),
        QUIZ(r"When $[\mathrm{X}]$ is trebled and nothing else changes, the initial rate becomes nine times as large. What is the order with respect to X?",
             ["0", "1", "2", "3"], 2, r"$3^m = 9$, so $m = 2$.",
             feedback=["Order 0 would leave the rate unchanged.", "Order 1 would give a factor of 3.", "", r"$3^3 = 27$, not 9."], hint=r"Solve $3^m = 9$."),
        QUIZ(r"In a clock reaction, doubling the concentration of one reactant halves the time for the colour change. What is the order with respect to that reactant?",
             ["0", "1", "2", "Half"], 1, r"Time halves, so $1/t$ doubles, so the rate doubles. Order 1.",
             feedback=["Order 0 would leave the time unchanged.", "", r"Order 2 would reduce the time to a quarter.", "Orders are not fractions of the time change."], hint=r"Rate is proportional to $1/t$."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Explain why the initial rate, and not the rate at some later time, is used in this method.",
           r"At $t = 0$ the concentrations are the known starting values, and there are no products yet that could slow the reaction or take part in it. Later the concentrations change and the rate falls, so we could not tell which concentration produced which rate."),
        PQ(r"The initial rate of $\mathrm{X} + \mathrm{Y} \rightarrow$ products is $3.0\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$ when $[\mathrm{X}] = 0.020$ and $[\mathrm{Y}] = 0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. Doubling $[\mathrm{X}]$ gives $6.0\times10^{-5}$. Trebling $[\mathrm{Y}]$ (with $[\mathrm{X}] = 0.020$) gives $2.7\times10^{-4}$. Find the rate equation, and $k$ with units.",
           r"Doubling X doubles the rate, so order 1. Trebling Y gives $\frac{2.7\times10^{-4}}{3.0\times10^{-5}} = 9 = 3^2$, so order 2. Rate $= k[\mathrm{X}][\mathrm{Y}]^2$. $k = \dfrac{3.0\times10^{-5}}{0.020\times(0.010)^2} = 15\ \mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"Using the rate equation from the question above, find the initial rate when $[\mathrm{X}] = 0.050$ and $[\mathrm{Y}] = 0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$.",
           r"$\text{rate} = 15 \times 0.050 \times (0.020)^2 = 15 \times 0.050 \times 4.0\times10^{-4} = 3.0\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ("Describe how a clock reaction can be used to find the order with respect to one reactant.",
           r"Keep the temperature and every other concentration fixed. Change the concentration of that reactant to several values (for example by dilution with water, keeping the total volume constant). Measure the time $t$ for the fixed colour change each time. Work out $1/t$ as the relative rate, then see how $1/t$ changes when the concentration is doubled: unchanged means order 0, doubled means order 1, quadrupled means order 2."),
        TIP("In the exam: a table of initial rates is a very common Paper 2 item and also appears as a multiple-choice item in Paper 1. The practical Paper 3 may ask you to plan or analyse a clock reaction."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("The initial rate is measured at t = 0, when concentrations are known and no products have built up.",
            r"Change one concentration at a time. Concentration factor $f$ and rate factor $g$ give the order $m$ from $f^m = g$.",
            r"Then write $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^m[\mathrm{B}]^n$ and find $k$ from any experiment, with units.",
            r"In a clock reaction, relative rate is proportional to $1/t$, not to $t$.",
            "Check the rate equation against a second experiment."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Half-life of first order reactions
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="Initial rates and half-life",
    title="Half-life and first order reactions",
    slug="chu1-half-life-first-order",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Use half-life to recognise first order reactions, use t1/2 = ln 2 / k, calculate concentrations at any time, and find k from the gradient of a graph of ln[A] against time.",
    objectives=[
        "Define half-life and recognise a constant half-life as the sign of a first order reaction",
        "Use t1/2 = ln 2 / k and the integrated form ln[A] = ln[A]0 - kt",
        "Find k from the gradient of a graph of ln[A] against time",
        "State how half-life behaves for zero and second order reactions",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Half-life", "First order", "ln[A] against t", "Integrated rate equation", "Radioactive decay"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Hospitals in Cameroon use medicines that the body removes steadily. Doctors talk about how long it takes for the amount in the blood to fall to half. That time is called the **half-life**. Chemists use the same idea for reactions."),
        D(r"The **half-life** $t_{1/2}$ of a reactant is the time taken for its concentration to fall to half of its value at the start of that period.", title="Half-life"),
        H("First order: a constant half-life"),
        T(r"For a **first order** reaction, $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]$. Look at what this means: when half of A is left, the rate is half as fast, so it takes the same time again to lose another half. The half-life is **constant**, whatever the starting concentration. This is the test for first order."),
        D(r"For a first order reaction the half-life depends only on $k$:", title="Half-life of a first order reaction",
          formula=r"t_{1/2} = \frac{\ln 2}{k} = \frac{0.693}{k}",
          note="A large k means a short half-life. Radioactive decay is also first order, which is why it has a constant half-life."),
        T(r"The same rate equation can be written as a straight-line relationship. Using calculus (separating the variables in $-\frac{d[\mathrm{A}]}{dt} = k[\mathrm{A}]$ and integrating) gives the integrated rate equation below. You are asked to use it, not to derive it."),
        D(r"For a first order reaction:", title="Integrated rate equation",
          formula=r"\ln[\mathrm{A}] = \ln[\mathrm{A}]_0 - kt \qquad\text{or}\qquad [\mathrm{A}] = [\mathrm{A}]_0\,e^{-kt}",
          note="ln is the natural logarithm. A graph of ln[A] against t is a straight line with gradient -k and intercept ln[A]0."),
        EX("Worked example 1: using the half-life", intro=r"A first order reaction has $t_{1/2} = 25.0$ minutes. (a) Calculate $k$. (b) What fraction of the reactant is left after 75.0 minutes?", steps=[
            S("(a) Rearrange", math=r"k = \frac{\ln 2}{t_{1/2}} = \frac{0.6931}{25.0}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"k = 0.0277\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}"),
            S("(b) Count half-lives", math=r"\frac{75.0}{25.0} = 3 \text{ half-lives}"),
            S("Fraction left", math=r"\left(\tfrac12\right)^3 = \tfrac18 = 0.125"),
        ], check=r"e^{-0.027726\times75.0} = e^{-2.0794} = 0.125"),
        H("Calculations with the integrated equation"),
        EX("Worked example 2: concentration and time", intro=r"A first order reaction has $k = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $[\mathrm{A}]_0 = 0.80\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. (a) Find $[\mathrm{A}]$ after 500 s. (b) How long until $[\mathrm{A}] = 0.10$?", steps=[
            S("(a) Use the exponential form", math=r"[\mathrm{A}] = 0.80\,e^{-(2.0\times10^{-3})(500)} = 0.80\,e^{-1.0}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"[\mathrm{A}] = 0.80 \times 0.3679 = 0.294\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("(b) Rearrange the log form", math=r"t = \frac{1}{k}\ln\frac{[\mathrm{A}]_0}{[\mathrm{A}]} = \frac{1}{2.0\times10^{-3}}\ln\frac{0.80}{0.10}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"t = 500 \times \ln 8 = 500 \times 2.0794 = 1.04\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{s}"),
        ], check=r"\ln 8 = 3\ln 2 \text{, so } t = 3\times\frac{\ln 2}{k} = 3 \times 346.6 = 1040\ \mathrm{s}"),
        TIP(r"The ratio $0.80 \to 0.10$ is three halvings ($0.80 \to 0.40 \to 0.20 \to 0.10$). Spotting this gives a quick check: three half-lives."),
        H("Finding k from a graph of ln[A] against time"),
        T(r"Because $\ln[\mathrm{A}] = \ln[\mathrm{A}]_0 - kt$ has the form $y = c + mx$, plotting $\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ (y) against $t$ (x) gives a straight line **only for a first order reaction**. The gradient is $-k$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: from data to k and half-life", intro=r"In an experiment (teaching data) $[\mathrm{A}]$ was recorded at times: 0 s, 0.800; 100 s, 0.536; 200 s, 0.359; 300 s, 0.241; 400 s, 0.162 (mol per dm3). Show that the reaction is first order and find $k$ and $t_{1/2}$.", steps=[
            S("Take natural logs", text="ln[A] values are -0.223, -0.624, -1.024, -1.423, -1.820. They fall by about 0.40 every 100 s."),
            S("A constant fall means a straight line, so first order", text="The graph of ln[A] against t is straight."),
            S("Gradient", math=r"\frac{-1.820 - (-0.223)}{400 - 0} = -4.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}"),
            S("k is minus the gradient", math=r"k = 4.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}"),
            S("Half-life", math=r"t_{1/2} = \frac{0.693}{4.0\times10^{-3}} = 173\ \mathrm{s}"),
        ], check=r"0.800 \to 0.400\ \text{takes}\ 173\ \mathrm{s};\ 0.400 \to 0.200\ \text{another}\ 173\ \mathrm{s}"),
        H("Other orders"),
        KEY("Half-life for each order", "Compare how t1/2 changes as the reaction proceeds.", cards=[
            ["Zero order", r"$t_{1/2} = \dfrac{[\mathrm{A}]_0}{2k}$. It gets shorter as [A] falls."],
            ["First order", r"$t_{1/2} = \dfrac{\ln 2}{k}$. It is constant."],
            ["Second order", r"$t_{1/2} = \dfrac{1}{k[\mathrm{A}]_0}$. It gets longer as [A] falls."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using log10 instead of ln", r"The equations above use the natural logarithm. If you use $\log_{10}$ you must change the constants. A calculator key marked ln is what you need."),
        MISTAKE("Taking the gradient as k", r"The graph of $\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ against $t$ slopes downwards, so the gradient is negative. $k$ is the positive number $-\text{gradient}$."),
        MISTAKE("Using half-life as a test for order from a single value", r"One half-life tells you nothing. You must show that the half-life is the same for two or more successive halvings."),
        QUIZ(r"A first order reaction has $t_{1/2} = 40$ s. What percentage of the reactant remains after 120 s?",
             ["50%", "25%", "12.5%", "6.25%"], 2, r"120 s is 3 half-lives, so $\tfrac18 = 12.5\%$ remains.",
             feedback=["That is after one half-life.", "That is after two half-lives.", "", "That is after four half-lives (160 s)."], hint="Divide the time by the half-life."),
        QUIZ(r"Which graph is a straight line for a first order reaction?",
             [r"$[\mathrm{A}]$ against $t$", r"$\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ against $t$", r"$1/[\mathrm{A}]$ against $t$", r"$\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ against $1/t$"], 1,
             r"$\ln[\mathrm{A}] = \ln[\mathrm{A}]_0 - kt$ is a straight line in $t$.",
             feedback=["This is a straight line only for zero order.", "", "This is a straight line for second order.", "This is not any standard rate graph."], hint="Match the integrated equation to y = c + mx."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The half-life of a first order reaction is 346.6 s. Calculate $k$ and the time for the concentration to fall to $\tfrac1{16}$ of its original value.",
           r"$k = \dfrac{0.6931}{346.6} = 2.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$. $\tfrac1{16} = (\tfrac12)^4$, so the time is $4 \times 346.6 = 1386\ \mathrm{s}$ (about $1.39\times10^3$ s)."),
        PQ(r"A first order reaction has $k = 4.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Calculate $t_{1/2}$ and the concentration after 300 s if $[\mathrm{A}]_0 = 0.800\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$.",
           r"$t_{1/2} = \dfrac{0.693}{4.0\times10^{-3}} = 173\ \mathrm{s}$. $[\mathrm{A}] = 0.800\,e^{-0.0040\times300} = 0.800\,e^{-1.2} = 0.800\times0.3012 = 0.241\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$."),
        PQ("A student measures the concentration of a reactant and finds that it takes 120 s to fall from 0.80 to 0.40 and another 120 s to fall from 0.40 to 0.20 mol per dm3. State and explain the order of the reaction, and calculate k.",
           r"The half-life is constant (120 s) for successive halvings, so the reaction is first order. $k = \dfrac{0.693}{120} = 5.8\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$."),
        PQ("Describe how you would use a graph to show that a reaction is first order and to find its rate constant.",
           r"Measure $[\mathrm{A}]$ at regular times, calculate $\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ and plot it (y) against time (x). A straight line shows first order. The gradient equals $-k$, so $k$ is the negative of the gradient. Alternatively, read two or more successive half-lives from the concentration-time graph: if they are equal the reaction is first order."),
        TIP("In the exam: half-life and the first order integrated equation are tested in Paper 2 structured questions (usually with a graph or table). Paper 1 may ask for a quick number of half-lives."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Half-life is the time for a concentration to halve. A constant half-life shows first order.",
            r"First order: $t_{1/2} = \ln 2 / k$, and $[\mathrm{A}] = [\mathrm{A}]_0 e^{-kt}$.",
            r"A graph of $\ln[\mathrm{A}]$ against $t$ is a straight line with gradient $-k$ only for first order.",
            r"Zero order: $t_{1/2}$ decreases as the reaction proceeds. Second order: $t_{1/2}$ increases.",
            "Use natural logs (ln) in these equations."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. Mechanisms and rate-determining step
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="Mechanisms and the rate-determining step",
    title="Reaction mechanisms and the rate-determining step",
    slug="chu1-mechanisms-rds",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Describe a mechanism as a series of elementary steps, identify intermediates and the rate-determining step, and link the rate equation to a proposed mechanism.",
    objectives=[
        "Define elementary step, intermediate, molecularity and rate-determining step",
        "Check that the steps of a mechanism add up to the overall equation",
        "Deduce the rate equation from a mechanism, including a fast step before the slow step",
        "Decide whether a proposed mechanism is consistent with an experimental rate equation",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Elementary step", "Intermediate", "Rate-determining step", "Molecularity", "Consistent mechanism"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"On a busy road to a market in Bafoussam, traffic moves only as fast as the narrowest, slowest stretch. Cars that are quick elsewhere cannot go faster than that bottleneck. A reaction with several steps behaves in the same way. The slowest step controls the overall rate."),
        H("Elementary steps and intermediates"),
        T(r"Most reactions do not happen in one collision in the way the balanced equation suggests. They occur as a series of simple stages called **elementary steps**. The set of steps is the **mechanism**."),
        D(r"An **elementary step** is a single stage of a mechanism, a collision or breakdown of one, two (rarely three) particles. The number of particles in the step is its **molecularity**: unimolecular (one particle) or bimolecular (two).", title="Elementary step"),
        D(r"An **intermediate** is a species that is formed in one step and used up in a later step. It does not appear in the overall equation. A **catalyst** is used in an early step and regenerated in a later one. A **transition state** is the unstable arrangement at the top of an energy barrier. It is not an intermediate; it cannot be isolated.", title="Intermediate, catalyst and transition state"),
        D(r"The **rate-determining step** (rds) is the slowest step in the mechanism. It has the highest activation energy. The overall rate cannot be faster than the rds.", title="Rate-determining step"),
        IMG("al-chu1-two-step-profile.svg", alt="Energy profile for a two-step reaction. Reactants rise over a high first peak to a valley (the intermediate), then over a lower second peak down to the products. The first step has the larger activation energy, so it is the slow step.", caption="Figure 1. Energy profile of a two-step mechanism: the step with the higher barrier is rate-determining."),
        H("From mechanism to rate equation"),
        KEY("Rules for a consistent mechanism", "A proposed mechanism must pass all three tests.", cards=[
            ["Sum", "The steps must add up to the overall balanced equation. Intermediates cancel."],
            ["Rate equation", "The reactants in the rate-determining step (and any species formed in fast steps before it) are the species in the rate equation, each to the power of its number in that step."],
            ["Proposal only", "Agreeing with the rate equation does not prove a mechanism. Another mechanism may agree too."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: nitrogen dioxide and carbon monoxide", intro=r"The reaction $\mathrm{NO_2} + \mathrm{CO} \rightarrow \mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{CO_2}$ has the experimental rate equation $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{NO_2}]^2$. A mechanism is proposed: Step 1 (slow): $2\mathrm{NO_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{NO_3} + \mathrm{NO}$. Step 2 (fast): $\mathrm{NO_3} + \mathrm{CO} \rightarrow \mathrm{NO_2} + \mathrm{CO_2}$. Show that it is consistent.", steps=[
            S("Add the steps", math=r"2\mathrm{NO_2} + \mathrm{NO_3} + \mathrm{CO} \rightarrow \mathrm{NO_3} + \mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{NO_2} + \mathrm{CO_2}"),
            S("Cancel $\\mathrm{NO_3}$ (the intermediate) and one $\\mathrm{NO_2}$", math=r"\mathrm{NO_2} + \mathrm{CO} \rightarrow \mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{CO_2}"),
            S("Rate equation from the slow step", math=r"\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{NO_2}]^2"),
            S("Compare with experiment", text="The slow step has two NO2 molecules, so second order in NO2. CO takes part only after the slow step, so it does not appear. This agrees with the experiment."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: two ways of hydrolysing a bromoalkane", intro=r"Compare (a) 2-bromo-2-methylpropane, $(\mathrm{CH_3})_3\mathrm{CBr}$, and (b) bromoethane, $\mathrm{C_2H_5Br}$, reacting with hydroxide ions. Experiments give (a) $\text{rate} = k[(\mathrm{CH_3})_3\mathrm{CBr}]$ and (b) $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{C_2H_5Br}][\mathrm{OH^-}]$. Suggest the mechanism type.", steps=[
            S("(a) First order, hydroxide absent from the rate equation", text="The slow step is the breaking of the C-Br bond on its own: $(\\mathrm{CH_3})_3\\mathrm{CBr} \\rightarrow (\\mathrm{CH_3})_3\\mathrm{C^+} + \\mathrm{Br^-}$. The fast step follows: $(\\mathrm{CH_3})_3\\mathrm{C^+} + \\mathrm{OH^-} \\rightarrow (\\mathrm{CH_3})_3\\mathrm{COH}$. This is the $S_N1$ route; the carbocation is the intermediate."),
            S("(b) First order in each reactant", text="One bimolecular step in which $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ attacks the carbon as the bromide leaves. This is the $S_N2$ route, with no intermediate, only a transition state."),
            S("The link", text="The rate equation shows which species are in the slow step. This is how mechanisms are proposed."),
        ]),
        H("A fast step before the slow step"),
        T(r"An intermediate must not appear in a rate equation, because we cannot measure it easily. If the slow step uses an intermediate that was made in a fast **equilibrium** step, we replace the intermediate using the equilibrium constant."),
        EX("Worked example 3: nitrogen monoxide and oxygen", intro=r"$2\mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{NO_2}$ has experimental rate $= k[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}]$. A mechanism: Step 1 (fast equilibrium): $2\mathrm{NO} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{N_2O_2}$. Step 2 (slow): $\mathrm{N_2O_2} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{NO_2}$. Show that it is consistent.", steps=[
            S("Sum of the steps", math=r"2\mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{N_2O_2} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{N_2O_2} + 2\mathrm{NO_2} \;\Rightarrow\; 2\mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{NO_2}"),
            S("Rate from the slow step", math=r"\text{rate} = k_2[\mathrm{N_2O_2}][\mathrm{O_2}]"),
            S("Equilibrium constant of step 1", math=r"K = \frac{[\mathrm{N_2O_2}]}{[\mathrm{NO}]^2} \;\Rightarrow\; [\mathrm{N_2O_2}] = K[\mathrm{NO}]^2"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"\text{rate} = k_2K[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}] = k[\mathrm{NO}]^2[\mathrm{O_2}]"),
            S("Conclusion", text="The mechanism gives the experimental rate equation, so it is consistent with it."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Putting an intermediate in the rate equation", r"The final rate equation may contain only reactants (and catalysts), not intermediates. Replace an intermediate using the fast equilibrium before the slow step."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the rate equation proves the mechanism", r"Different mechanisms can give the same rate equation. Say the mechanism is *consistent with* the data, not that it is proved."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing an intermediate with a transition state", r"An intermediate sits in a valley on the energy profile and could in principle be detected. A transition state is a peak and cannot be isolated."),
        QUIZ(r"In a two-step mechanism the first step is slow and the second is fast. The first step is $\mathrm{A} + \mathrm{B} \rightarrow \mathrm{X}$. What is the rate equation?",
             [r"$\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{X}]$", r"$\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{B}]$", r"$\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}]^2[\mathrm{B}]$", r"$\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{B}][\mathrm{X}]$"], 1,
             r"The slow step has one A and one B, so rate $= k[\mathrm{A}][\mathrm{B}]$.",
             feedback=["X is an intermediate, so it cannot be in the rate equation.", "", "A appears only once in the slow step, so its power is 1.", "Intermediates do not appear, and X is a product of the slow step."], hint="Write the rate equation for the slow step only."),
        QUIZ("Which of these is an intermediate?",
             ["A species used in the first step and regenerated in the last step", "A species formed in one step and used up in a later step", "The unstable arrangement at the top of an energy barrier", "A reactant that appears in the overall equation"], 1,
             "An intermediate is made and then used up, so it cancels in the overall equation.",
             feedback=["That describes a catalyst.", "", "That is a transition state.", "Intermediates do not appear in the overall equation."], hint="Think of what cancels when the steps are added."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is catalysed by iodide ions. Proposed mechanism: Step 1 (slow): $\mathrm{H_2O_2} + \mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2O} + \mathrm{IO^-}$. Step 2 (fast): $\mathrm{H_2O_2} + \mathrm{IO^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2O} + \mathrm{O_2} + \mathrm{I^-}$. Write the overall equation, identify the intermediate and the catalyst, and state the rate equation.",
           r"Overall: $2\mathrm{H_2O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2O} + \mathrm{O_2}$. Intermediate: $\mathrm{IO^-}$ (formed in step 1, used in step 2). Catalyst: $\mathrm{I^-}$ (used in step 1, regenerated in step 2). Rate $= k[\mathrm{H_2O_2}][\mathrm{I^-}]$, from the slow step."),
        PQ(r"The iodination of propanone, $\mathrm{CH_3COCH_3} + \mathrm{I_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COCH_2I} + \mathrm{HI}$, is catalysed by acid. The experimental rate equation is $\text{rate} = k[\mathrm{CH_3COCH_3}][\mathrm{H^+}]$. What does this tell you about the rate-determining step and about iodine?",
           r"The rate-determining step involves one propanone molecule and one $\mathrm{H^+}$ ion (the formation of an intermediate called the enol). Iodine is zero order: it does not appear in the rate equation, so it reacts only in a fast step after the slow step. $\mathrm{H^+}$ appears in the rate equation but not in the overall equation, so it is a catalyst."),
        PQ(r"A reaction $\mathrm{X} + 2\mathrm{Y} \rightarrow \mathrm{Z}$ has rate $= k[\mathrm{X}][\mathrm{Y}]$. Suggest a two-step mechanism that is consistent, and explain why one-step collision of X with two Y molecules is unlikely.",
           r"Step 1 (slow): $\mathrm{X} + \mathrm{Y} \rightarrow \mathrm{I}$ (intermediate). Step 2 (fast): $\mathrm{I} + \mathrm{Y} \rightarrow \mathrm{Z}$. The steps add to $\mathrm{X} + 2\mathrm{Y} \rightarrow \mathrm{Z}$, and the slow step gives rate $= k[\mathrm{X}][\mathrm{Y}]$. A single collision of three particles is very unlikely, and would give rate $= k[\mathrm{X}][\mathrm{Y}]^2$, which disagrees with experiment."),
        PQ("Draw (in words) the energy profile for a two-step reaction and show where the rate-determining step is.",
           "The profile starts at the reactants, rises to a first peak, falls into a valley (the intermediate), rises to a second peak and falls to the products. The step with the higher peak above its starting level has the higher activation energy. It is the slower, rate-determining step."),
        TIP("In the exam: mechanisms and the rate-determining step are tested in Paper 2 (given an experimental rate equation, suggest or test a mechanism) and as multiple-choice items in Paper 1. The same ideas appear in organic nucleophilic substitution (SN1 and SN2)."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A mechanism is a set of elementary steps. Intermediates are formed and then used up; catalysts are used and regenerated.",
            "The rate-determining step is the slowest step and has the highest activation energy.",
            "The rate equation contains the species in the rate-determining step (and fast steps before it), not intermediates.",
            "The steps must add to the overall equation. Agreeing with the rate equation does not prove a mechanism.",
            r"For a fast equilibrium before the slow step, replace the intermediate using $K$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. Arrhenius equation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="The Arrhenius equation",
    title="The Arrhenius equation and activation energy",
    slug="chu1-arrhenius",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use k = A e^(-Ea/RT) and its logarithmic form to link rate constant and temperature, find Ea from a plot of ln k against 1/T or from two values of k, and predict how k changes with T.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the effect of temperature on rate using the Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies",
        "Use k = A e^(-Ea/RT) and ln k = ln A - Ea/RT",
        "Find Ea from the gradient of a graph of ln k against 1/T",
        "Use ln(k2/k1) = (Ea/R)(1/T1 - 1/T2) with two temperatures",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Activation energy", "Boltzmann distribution", "Arrhenius equation", "Pre-exponential factor A", "ln k against 1/T"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Fish left on a market stall in Limbe spoils within hours in the midday heat, but lasts much longer in a cold box. The reactions of decay are the same; only the temperature differs. Why does a small rise in temperature increase the rate so much? And can we measure how sensitive a reaction is to temperature? The **Arrhenius equation** answers both questions."),
        H("Why temperature matters: the Boltzmann distribution"),
        T(r"For two particles to react they must collide with at least a minimum energy, the **activation energy** $E_a$, and in a suitable orientation. In a gas or solution the particles have a wide spread of energies. Only the particles in the high-energy tail have $E \geq E_a$."),
        IMG("al-chu1-boltzmann.svg", alt="Two curves of number of molecules against energy. The higher temperature curve is lower and broader, with a longer tail. A vertical dashed line marks the activation energy, and the area under each curve beyond it is shaded; it is larger for the higher temperature.", caption="Figure 1. Raising the temperature moves more molecules above the activation energy. The total number of molecules does not change."),
        T(r"A rise of just $10\ \mathrm{K}$ does not make collisions much more frequent, but it makes the fraction of molecules with $E \geq E_a$ much larger. This is the main reason that the rate increases quickly with temperature."),
        H("The Arrhenius equation"),
        D(r"The rate constant $k$ depends on temperature $T$ (in kelvin) as", title="Arrhenius equation",
          formula=r"k = A\,e^{-E_a/RT}",
          note="A is the pre-exponential factor (same units as k). Ea is in joules per mole. R = 8.31 J per K per mol. The factor e^(-Ea/RT) is roughly the fraction of molecules with energy at least Ea."),
        T(r"Take natural logs of both sides. This turns the equation into a straight line:"),
        F(r"\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{R}\cdot\frac{1}{T}"),
        T(r"This has the form $y = c + mx$ with $y = \ln k$, $x = 1/T$, gradient $m = -E_a/R$ and intercept $c = \ln A$. A graph of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ gives a straight line sloping downwards, and $E_a = -R \times \text{gradient}$. If you have values at **two temperatures**, subtract the two equations:"),
        D(r"With rate constants $k_1$ at $T_1$ and $k_2$ at $T_2$ (kelvin):", title="Two-temperature form",
          formula=r"\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}\right)"),
        IMG("al-chu1-arrhenius-plot.svg", alt="Graph of ln k against 1/T in units of 10 to the power minus 3 per kelvin. Six points lie on a straight line sloping down from about 4.1 at 1/T = 2.94 to about 0.4 at 1/T = 3.45. A dashed triangle shows delta x = 0.40 and delta y = minus 2.89.", caption="Figure 2. An Arrhenius plot. The gradient is -Ea/R. Teaching data."),
        EX("Worked example 1: Ea from the gradient", intro=r"On the Arrhenius plot in Figure 2 (teaching data) the line passes through $(3.00,\ 3.67)$ and $(3.40,\ 0.78)$, where the horizontal values are $1/T$ in units of $10^{-3}\ \mathrm{K^{-1}}$. Calculate $E_a$.", steps=[
            S("Gradient (convert the axis units)", math=r"\frac{0.78 - 3.67}{(3.40 - 3.00)\times10^{-3}} = \frac{-2.89}{0.40\times10^{-3}} = -7.2\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{K}"),
            S("Use gradient $= -E_a/R$", math=r"E_a = -R \times \text{gradient} = 8.31 \times 7.225\times10^{3}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"E_a = 6.0\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = 60.0\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"7225 \times 8.31 = 60\,040\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: Ea from two rate constants", intro=r"A reaction has $k_1 = 1.5\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $298\ \mathrm{K}$ and $k_2 = 6.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $313\ \mathrm{K}$. Calculate $E_a$ (take $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$).", steps=[
            S("Left-hand side", math=r"\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \ln 4 = 1.3863"),
            S("Temperature term", math=r"\frac{1}{298} - \frac{1}{313} = 3.3557\times10^{-3} - 3.1949\times10^{-3} = 1.608\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{K^{-1}}"),
            S("Rearrange for Ea", math=r"E_a = \frac{R\ln(k_2/k_1)}{1/T_1 - 1/T_2} = \frac{8.31 \times 1.3863}{1.608\times10^{-4}}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"E_a = 7.16\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = 71.6\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
        ], check=r"\exp\!\left[\frac{71\,635}{8.31}(1.608\times10^{-4})\right] = e^{1.3863} = 4.0"),
        MISTAKE("Using degrees Celsius", r"Temperatures in the Arrhenius equation must be in kelvin: add 273 to the Celsius value. Using $25$ and $40$ instead of $298$ and $313$ gives a completely wrong answer."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing joules and kilojoules", r"$R$ is in joules, so $E_a$ must be in joules per mole in the equation. Convert kJ to J (multiply by 1000) before substituting, and convert your answer back to kJ."),
        MISTAKE("Dropping the minus sign on the gradient", r"The gradient of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ is $-E_a/R$, a negative number. $E_a$ is always positive."),
        EX("Worked example 3: how much faster at a higher temperature?", intro=r"A reaction has $E_a = 50.0\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. By what factor does $k$ increase when the temperature rises from $300\ \mathrm{K}$ to $310\ \mathrm{K}$?", steps=[
            S("Use the two-temperature form", math=r"\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \frac{50\,000}{8.31}\left(\frac{1}{300} - \frac{1}{310}\right)"),
            S("Evaluate the bracket", math=r"\frac{1}{300} - \frac{1}{310} = 1.0753\times10^{-4}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = 6017 \times 1.0753\times10^{-4} = 0.6470"),
            S("Take the exponential", math=r"\frac{k_2}{k_1} = e^{0.6470} = 1.91"),
        ]),
        T(r"So the rate nearly doubles. That is where the rule of thumb that a 10 degree rise doubles the rate comes from. It is only a rough guide: it is true when $E_a$ is about $50\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ near room temperature. A reaction with a larger $E_a$ is more sensitive to temperature."),
        QUIZ(r"A graph of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ has a gradient of $-5.0\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{K}$. What is $E_a$?",
             [r"$5.0\times10^{3}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$", r"$-41.6\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$", r"$41.6\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$", r"$0.60\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$"], 2,
             r"$E_a = -R \times \text{gradient} = 8.31 \times 5.0\times10^{3} = 41\,550\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = 41.6\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.",
             feedback=["You did not multiply by R.", "Activation energy is positive. The minus sign is already in the gradient.", "", r"You divided by R instead of multiplying: $5.0\times10^{3}/8.31 = 602\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$."], hint=r"Gradient $= -E_a/R$."),
        QUIZ("Why does a small rise in temperature greatly increase the rate of reaction?",
             ["The activation energy falls", "The fraction of molecules with energy at least Ea rises a lot", "The total number of molecules increases", "The reaction becomes less exothermic"], 1,
             "The activation energy is unchanged, but many more molecules have enough energy.",
             feedback=["Temperature does not change Ea; a catalyst does.", "", "The number of molecules stays the same.", "The enthalpy change of reaction is not affected in this way."], hint="Think of the tail of the energy distribution."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The rate constant of a reaction is $2.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $300\ \mathrm{K}$ and $3.5\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $330\ \mathrm{K}$. Calculate $E_a$ in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.",
           r"$\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \ln 17.5 = 2.8622$. $\frac{1}{300} - \frac{1}{330} = 3.0303\times10^{-4}$. $E_a = \frac{8.31 \times 2.8622}{3.0303\times10^{-4}} = 7.85\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = 78.5\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain, using ideas about energy, why $k$ increases with temperature even though the activation energy does not change.",
           r"At a higher temperature a bigger fraction of molecules have energy at least equal to $E_a$ (the tail of the Boltzmann distribution grows). So a larger fraction of collisions are successful and the rate constant rises. The activation energy is a property of the reaction pathway and is unchanged."),
        PQ(r"Describe how you would find the activation energy of a reaction from experiments.",
           r"Measure $k$ at several different temperatures (for example from initial rates or a clock reaction), keeping concentrations the same. Convert the temperatures to kelvin and calculate $\ln k$ and $1/T$. Plot $\ln k$ against $1/T$ and draw the best straight line. The gradient equals $-E_a/R$, so $E_a = -8.31 \times \text{gradient}$, converted to $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"A reaction has $E_a = 75.0\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Calculate the factor by which the rate constant increases from $298\ \mathrm{K}$ to $308\ \mathrm{K}$.",
           r"$\ln\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \frac{75\,000}{8.31}\left(\frac{1}{298} - \frac{1}{308}\right) = 9025 \times 1.0895\times10^{-4} = 0.9833$. $\frac{k_2}{k_1} = e^{0.9833} = 2.67$."),
        TIP("In the exam: the Arrhenius equation, its logarithmic form and the graph of ln k against 1/T are Paper 2 calculation items. Paper 1 may ask you to read a gradient or recall why temperature affects rate. Always convert to kelvin and joules."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Only molecules with $E \geq E_a$ can react; a small rise in $T$ greatly increases this fraction.",
            r"Arrhenius: $k = A e^{-E_a/RT}$, so $\ln k = \ln A - E_a/(RT)$.",
            r"A graph of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ is a straight line with gradient $-E_a/R$ and intercept $\ln A$.",
            r"Two temperatures: $\ln(k_2/k_1) = (E_a/R)(1/T_1 - 1/T_2)$.",
            "Use kelvin, joules per mole and R = 8.31 J per K per mol."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. Catalysis
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=KIN, topic="Catalysis",
    title="Homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis",
    slug="chu1-catalysis",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Explain how catalysts lower the activation energy by an alternative pathway, distinguish homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis with examples, and state what a catalyst does not change.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain catalysis in terms of an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy",
        "Describe homogeneous catalysis (for example Fe2+/Fe3+ with peroxodisulfate and iodide) and heterogeneous catalysis (adsorption, reaction, desorption)",
        "State what a catalyst does and does not change (rate, Ea, delta H, K)",
        "Calculate the effect of a lower activation energy on the rate constant",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Catalyst", "Alternative pathway", "Homogeneous catalysis", "Heterogeneous catalysis", "Adsorption", "Catalyst poisoning"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Most industrial chemistry and almost all of life depend on catalysts. The iron in the Haber process makes ammonia for fertiliser. Platinum and rhodium in a car's exhaust system convert poisonous gases to harmless ones. Enzymes in your saliva begin to digest the starch in cassava or plantain. In this lesson you will explain how catalysts work and what they cannot do."),
        D(r"A **catalyst** is a substance that increases the rate of a reaction by providing an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. It is regenerated at the end, so it is not used up in the overall reaction.", title="Catalyst"),
        IMG("al-chu1-catalyst-profile.svg", alt="Energy profile with two routes from reactants to products. The uncatalysed route (solid) has a high peak. The catalysed route (dashed) has a lower peak. Arrows show the two activation energies and the unchanged enthalpy change.", caption="Figure 1. A catalyst lowers the activation energy. Reactant and product energies, and so delta H, are unchanged."),
        KEY("What a catalyst changes, and what it does not", "Be exact in exam answers.", cards=[
            ["It changes", r"The route taken. The activation energy falls, so more collisions succeed, so the rate constant $k$ rises."],
            ["It does not change", r"The enthalpy change $\Delta H$, the equilibrium constant $K$, or the equilibrium yield. It speeds the forward and backward reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner."],
            ["It may be", "Physically changed (a solid catalyst can become coated or powdery) but chemically the same at the end."],
        ]),
        H("Homogeneous catalysis"),
        D(r"In **homogeneous catalysis** the catalyst is in the same phase as the reactants, for example all dissolved in water or all gases.", title="Homogeneous catalyst"),
        T(r"The reaction $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + \mathrm{I_2}$ is slow. Both reactants are negative ions, which repel each other. Adding a small amount of $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ or $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ ions speeds it up. Standard electrode potentials explain why it is possible: $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}/SO_4^{2-}}$ is $+2.01\ \mathrm{V}$, $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}}$ is $+0.77\ \mathrm{V}$ and $\mathrm{I_2/I^-}$ is $+0.54\ \mathrm{V}$. So $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ can oxidise iodide, and peroxodisulfate can oxidise $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the iron(II)/iron(III) mechanism", intro=r"Show that the two steps below add up to the uncatalysed equation and that iron is a catalyst. Step 1: $2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + \mathrm{I_2}$. Step 2: $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$.", steps=[
            S("Add the two steps", math=r"2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} + \mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + \mathrm{I_2} + 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}"),
            S("Cancel $\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ on both sides", math=r"\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + \mathrm{I_2}"),
            S("Check the charge", text="Left: 2- plus 2 times 1- is 4-. Right: 2 times 2- is 4-. Balanced."),
            S("Explain the speed-up", text="Each step is between ions of opposite charge (Fe3+ with I-, and S2O8 2- with Fe2+), so they attract rather than repel. The catalyst is used in step 1 and regenerated in step 2."),
        ]),
        T(r"A second example is **autocatalysis**: in the reaction of manganate(VII) with ethanedioate ions, the product $\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ catalyses the reaction. The reaction starts slowly and then speeds up as $\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ builds up. This is why a titration of this kind is begun with a few drops added slowly, with warming."),
        H("Heterogeneous catalysis"),
        D(r"In **heterogeneous catalysis** the catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants, usually a solid with liquid or gas reactants.", title="Heterogeneous catalyst"),
        T(r"The reaction happens on the surface of the solid in three stages. First, reactant molecules are **adsorbed**, held on the active sites of the surface, which weakens their bonds. Second, the adsorbed molecules react. Third, the products are **desorbed**, released from the surface, and the sites are free again."),
        CODE("""Process / reaction                         Catalyst
Haber: N2 + 3H2 <=> 2NH3                   iron
Contact: 2SO2 + O2 <=> 2SO3                vanadium(V) oxide, V2O5
Exhaust: 2CO + 2NO -> 2CO2 + N2            platinum, palladium, rhodium
Hydrogenation of alkenes                   nickel""", "text", title="Some heterogeneous catalysts"),
        T(r"Surface area matters: a finely divided or porous catalyst has more active sites. **Poisoning** happens when an impurity binds strongly to the active sites and stops reactants from adsorbing. Sulfur compounds poison the iron in the Haber process, and lead compounds poisoned the catalysts of cars that burned leaded petrol. The reactants are purified to protect the catalyst."),
        EX("Worked example 2: how much faster with a lower activation energy?", intro=r"A catalyst lowers the activation energy of a reaction from $100\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ to $75\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. By what factor does the rate constant increase at $500\ \mathrm{K}$? Assume $A$ is unchanged and take $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$.", steps=[
            S("Write $k$ for each route", math=r"k_{\text{cat}} = A e^{-E_{a,\text{cat}}/RT}, \qquad k_{\text{uncat}} = A e^{-E_{a}/RT}"),
            S("Divide", math=r"\frac{k_{\text{cat}}}{k_{\text{uncat}}} = e^{(E_a - E_{a,\text{cat}})/RT} = e^{25\,000/(8.31 \times 500)}"),
            S("Evaluate the exponent", math=r"\frac{25\,000}{4155} = 6.017"),
            S("Take the exponential", math=r"\frac{k_{\text{cat}}}{k_{\text{uncat}}} = e^{6.017} = 4.1\times10^{2}"),
        ], check=r"\ln(410) = 6.016"),
        T(r"So a drop of only $25\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ in activation energy makes the reaction about 400 times faster at this temperature. Small changes in $E_a$ make a big difference because $E_a$ is in the exponent."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a catalyst and the equilibrium", intro=r"In the Haber process $\mathrm{N_2} + 3\mathrm{H_2} \rightleftharpoons 2\mathrm{NH_3}$ ($\Delta H = -92\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$), a student says that the iron catalyst will give a higher percentage yield of ammonia. Is the student correct?", steps=[
            S("What the catalyst does", text="It lowers the activation energy of both the forward and the backward reaction by the same amount."),
            S("Effect on the equilibrium", text="Both rates rise by the same factor, so the equilibrium position and the equilibrium constant $K$ are unchanged."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The student is not correct. The catalyst allows equilibrium to be reached much faster (so a lower temperature can be used economically), but the percentage yield at equilibrium is the same."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying a catalyst increases the yield", r"A catalyst does not change the equilibrium position. It only changes how fast equilibrium is reached."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a catalyst provides energy or lowers the temperature", r"A catalyst provides a different route with a lower activation energy. The energy of reactants and products is the same, so $\Delta H$ is unchanged."),
        QUIZ("Which statement about a catalyst is correct?",
             ["It increases the enthalpy change of the reaction", "It increases the equilibrium yield", "It provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy", "It increases the average energy of the molecules"], 2,
             "A catalyst lowers the activation energy by an alternative route.",
             feedback=["Delta H is fixed by the energies of reactants and products.", "The equilibrium position is not changed.", "", "The temperature, and so the average energy of the molecules, is unchanged."], hint="Recall the energy profile in Figure 1."),
        QUIZ("A solid platinum catalyst converts gaseous carbon monoxide and nitrogen monoxide in an exhaust. This is",
             ["Homogeneous catalysis, because everything is a gas", "Heterogeneous catalysis, because the catalyst is in a different phase", "Autocatalysis", "Not catalysis, because platinum is a solid"], 1,
             "The catalyst is a solid and the reactants are gases, so it is heterogeneous.",
             feedback=["The catalyst is solid and the reactants are gases: different phases.", "", "Autocatalysis means a product acts as the catalyst.", "Solid catalysts are common."], hint="Compare the phase of the catalyst with that of the reactants."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Explain, with a labelled energy profile described in words, how a catalyst increases the rate of reaction.",
           r"The catalyst provides an alternative pathway. On the energy profile the catalysed route has a lower peak, so the activation energy is smaller. A larger fraction of molecules has energy of at least the new, lower activation energy, so more collisions are successful and the rate increases. The energies of reactants and products, and so $\Delta H$, are unchanged."),
        PQ(r"Describe the three stages of heterogeneous catalysis, using the iron catalyst in the Haber process as an example, and explain what catalyst poisoning is.",
           r"Stage 1: nitrogen and hydrogen molecules are adsorbed onto active sites on the iron surface, which weakens the bonds. Stage 2: the adsorbed atoms react to form ammonia. Stage 3: ammonia is desorbed, freeing the sites. Poisoning is when an impurity such as a sulfur compound binds to the active sites so that reactants cannot be adsorbed, and the catalyst loses its activity."),
        PQ(r"A catalyst lowers the activation energy of a reaction by $20\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Calculate the factor by which $k$ increases at $300\ \mathrm{K}$ (assume $A$ is unchanged, $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$).",
           r"Factor $= e^{20\,000/(8.31 \times 300)} = e^{8.022} = 3.0\times10^{3}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ ions catalyse the reaction between $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}}$ and $\mathrm{I^-}$, giving the two equations for the catalysed route.",
           r"$\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}}$ and $\mathrm{I^-}$ are both negative and repel one another, so the direct reaction is slow. With iron the route is: $2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + \mathrm{I_2}$, then $\mathrm{S_2O_8^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} + 2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$. Each step is between oppositely charged ions, so the activation energy is lower. Iron is regenerated, so it is a catalyst."),
        TIP("In the exam: catalysis is tested in Paper 2 (energy profiles, homogeneous and heterogeneous examples, poisoning) and in Paper 1 (what a catalyst does and does not change). It links to the Haber and Contact processes and to transition metal chemistry."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A catalyst gives an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy and is regenerated.",
            r"It increases $k$ but does not change $\Delta H$, $K$ or the equilibrium yield; it speeds both directions equally.",
            r"Homogeneous: same phase, intermediate steps (for example $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}/Fe^{3+}}$). Heterogeneous: solid surface, with adsorption, reaction and desorption.",
            "Poisons block active sites. Autocatalysis is when a product catalyses the reaction.",
            r"A lower $E_a$ gives a larger $k$ by the factor $e^{\Delta E_a/RT}$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. Acid-base theories and pH
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Acid-base theories and pH",
    title="Brønsted-Lowry and Lewis theories; pH of strong acids and bases",
    slug="chu1-acid-base-theories-ph",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Describe acids and bases using the Brønsted-Lowry and Lewis theories, identify conjugate pairs, and calculate the pH of strong acids and bases, including after dilution and mixing.",
    objectives=[
        "Define Brønsted-Lowry acids and bases, conjugate pairs and amphoteric species",
        "Define Lewis acids and bases and give examples",
        "Use pH = -log[H+], Kw = [H+][OH-] and pH + pOH = 14 (25 degrees C)",
        "Calculate the pH of strong acids and bases, after dilution and after mixing an acid with a base",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Brønsted-Lowry acid and base", "Conjugate pair", "Lewis acid and base", "pH", "Kw", "Strong acid"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Lemon juice, soap, the acid in a car battery and the stomach's hydrochloric acid are all described by a number: the pH. At O Level you learned that acids give $\mathrm{H^+}$ ions in water. At A Level the idea is made wider, so that it also explains reactions that happen without water, and so that we can calculate pH accurately."),
        H("Brønsted-Lowry theory"),
        D(r"A **Brønsted-Lowry acid** is a proton donor. A **Brønsted-Lowry base** is a proton acceptor. A proton is a hydrogen ion, $\mathrm{H^+}$.", title="Brønsted-Lowry theory"),
        T(r"An acid-base reaction is a transfer of a proton. When an acid loses a proton, what is left is its **conjugate base**. When a base gains a proton, it becomes its **conjugate acid**. Each equation has two **conjugate pairs**."),
        F(r"\underset{\text{acid 1}}{\mathrm{CH_3COOH}} + \underset{\text{base 2}}{\mathrm{H_2O}} \rightleftharpoons \underset{\text{base 1}}{\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}} + \underset{\text{acid 2}}{\mathrm{H_3O^+}}"),
        T(r"Here $\mathrm{CH_3COOH/CH_3COO^-}$ is one conjugate pair and $\mathrm{H_3O^+/H_2O}$ is the other. In water, $\mathrm{H^+}$ is really $\mathrm{H_3O^+}$ (the oxonium ion); we write $\mathrm{H^+(aq)}$ as a shorthand. A species that can act as an acid or a base is **amphoteric**: water (as in $\mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{NH_4^+} + \mathrm{OH^-}$, where water is the acid) and hydrogencarbonate $\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ are examples."),
        H("Lewis theory"),
        D(r"A **Lewis acid** is an electron-pair acceptor. A **Lewis base** is an electron-pair donor. The product is formed by a **dative covalent bond**.", title="Lewis theory"),
        T(r"Every Brønsted-Lowry base has a lone pair, so it is also a Lewis base. The Lewis idea is wider because the acid does not have to be $\mathrm{H^+}$. In $\mathrm{BF_3} + \mathrm{NH_3} \rightarrow \mathrm{F_3B{-}NH_3}$ the nitrogen lone pair is donated into the empty orbital on boron, so $\mathrm{BF_3}$ is the Lewis acid and $\mathrm{NH_3}$ is the Lewis base. Metal ions that form complexes, such as $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ accepting lone pairs from water or ammonia, are Lewis acids. The proton itself is a Lewis acid too."),
        KEY("Choosing the right theory", "Use the narrowest theory that explains the question.", cards=[
            ["Brønsted-Lowry", "Proton transfer; gives conjugate pairs."],
            ["Lewis", "Electron-pair transfer; includes reactions with no protons, such as complex formation and BF3 with NH3."],
        ]),
        H("pH and the ionic product of water"),
        D(r"$\text{pH} = -\log_{10}[\mathrm{H^+}]$, so $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 10^{-\text{pH}}$.", title="pH", formula=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}[\mathrm{H^+}]"),
        D(r"Water ionises very slightly, $\mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{OH^-}$. In any aqueous solution, the ionic product of water $K_w$ is constant at a given temperature. At $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$:", title="Ionic product of water",
          formula=r"K_w = [\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{OH^-}] = 1.00\times10^{-14}\ \mathrm{mol^2\,dm^{-6}}",
          note="Taking -log of both sides gives pH + pOH = 14 at 25 degrees C. K_w increases with temperature (the ionisation is endothermic), so at higher temperatures pure water has pH below 7 but is still neutral."),
        T(r"**Strong acids** (HCl, $\mathrm{HNO_3}$) ionise completely in water, so $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ equals the acid concentration. **Strong bases** (NaOH, KOH) are completely ionised, so $[\mathrm{OH^-}]$ equals the base concentration (times the number of $\mathrm{OH^-}$ in the formula for $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$). For a base, find $[\mathrm{OH^-}]$, then use $K_w$ to find $[\mathrm{H^+}]$, or find pOH and subtract from 14."),
        EX("Worked example 1: strong acid and strong base", intro=r"Calculate the pH of (a) $0.0250\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl, (b) $0.0050\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$. Use $K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$ at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$.", steps=[
            S("(a) HCl is a strong acid", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = 0.0250\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(0.0250) = 1.60"),
            S("(b) $\\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$ gives two $\\mathrm{OH^-}$", math=r"[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 2 \times 0.0050 = 0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pOH", math=r"\text{pOH} = -\log_{10}(0.010) = 2.00"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = 14.00 - 2.00 = 12.00"),
        ], check=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{0.010} = 1.0\times10^{-12},\ \ -\log(1.0\times10^{-12}) = 12.00"),
        EX("Worked example 2: dilution", intro=r"$25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl is diluted with water to $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$. Find the pH.", steps=[
            S("Moles of H+", math=r"n = 0.100 \times \frac{25.0}{1000} = 2.50\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("New concentration", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \frac{2.50\times10^{-3}}{1.00} = 2.50\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(2.50\times10^{-3}) = 2.60"),
        ], check=r"\text{A fortyfold dilution raises the pH by } \log_{10}40 = 1.60:\ 1.00 + 1.60 = 2.60"),
        EX("Worked example 3: mixing an acid with a base", intro=r"$40.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl is mixed with $10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Find the pH of the mixture.", steps=[
            S("Moles of each", math=r"n(\mathrm{H^+}) = 4.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}, \qquad n(\mathrm{OH^-}) = 1.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("They react 1 : 1, so find the excess", math=r"n(\mathrm{H^+})_{\text{left}} = 4.00\times10^{-3} - 1.00\times10^{-3} = 3.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Total volume is $50.0\\ \\mathrm{cm^3}$", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \frac{3.00\times10^{-3}}{0.0500} = 0.0600\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(0.0600) = 1.22"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that Ba(OH)2 gives two hydroxide ions", r"For $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$ and $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$, $[\mathrm{OH^-}]$ is twice the concentration of the base."),
        MISTAKE("Using the original volumes after mixing", "After mixing, divide the moles left over by the total volume, not by one of the original volumes."),
        QUIZ(r"In $\mathrm{HCO_3^-} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H_2CO_3} + \mathrm{OH^-}$, which two species are the Brønsted-Lowry acids (one on each side)?",
             [r"$\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ and $\mathrm{OH^-}$", r"$\mathrm{H_2O}$ and $\mathrm{H_2CO_3}$", r"$\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ and $\mathrm{H_2O}$", r"$\mathrm{OH^-}$ and $\mathrm{H_2CO_3}$"], 1,
             r"$\mathrm{H_2O}$ donates a proton in the forward reaction, and $\mathrm{H_2CO_3}$ donates one in the reverse reaction. $\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$ and $\mathrm{OH^-}$ are the bases.",
             feedback=["HCO3- accepts a proton in the forward reaction and OH- accepts one in the reverse reaction. Both are bases.", "", "HCO3- is acting as a base here.", "OH- is a base. H2CO3 is an acid."], hint="An acid is the species that donates a proton."),
        QUIZ(r"What is the pH of $0.0010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$?",
             ["3.00", "11.00", "10.00", "13.00"], 1, r"$\text{pOH} = 3.00$, so $\text{pH} = 14.00 - 3.00 = 11.00$.",
             feedback=["That is the pOH.", "", "You subtracted 4 instead of 3.", "You did not use the concentration."], hint="Find the pOH first."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"In each equation identify the Brønsted-Lowry acid and base on the left, and the conjugate pairs: $\mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{NH_4^+} + \mathrm{OH^-}$.",
           r"$\mathrm{NH_3}$ accepts a proton, so it is the base; $\mathrm{H_2O}$ donates one, so it is the acid. Conjugate pairs: $\mathrm{NH_4^+/NH_3}$ and $\mathrm{H_2O/OH^-}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ acts as a Lewis acid when it reacts with a chloride ion to form $\mathrm{AlCl_4^-}$.",
           r"The aluminium atom in $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ has only six electrons in its outer shell and an empty orbital. A chloride ion donates a lone pair into this orbital, forming a dative covalent bond. $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ is the electron-pair acceptor, so it is the Lewis acid."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of (a) $0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{HNO_3}$ and (b) $0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{Ba(OH)_2}$. ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$.)",
           r"(a) $\text{pH} = -\log(0.020) = 1.70$. (b) $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 0.040$, $\text{pOH} = -\log(0.040) = 1.40$, so $\text{pH} = 14.00 - 1.40 = 12.60$."),
        PQ(r"$20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH is added to $10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl and the solution is made up to $100\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ with water. Calculate the pH, showing your reasoning.",
           r"Moles of $\mathrm{OH^-} = 0.100\times0.0200 = 2.00\times10^{-3}$. Moles of $\mathrm{H^+} = 0.200\times0.0100 = 2.00\times10^{-3}$. They are equal, so they neutralise exactly. The solution is aqueous sodium chloride, a neutral salt, so $\text{pH} = 7.00$ at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: acid-base theories and strong-acid pH calculations appear in Paper 1 (multiple choice, quick pH calculations and conjugate pairs) and Paper 2 (structured questions). Always state Kw and the temperature."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Brønsted-Lowry: acids donate protons, bases accept them. Every acid has a conjugate base, and every base a conjugate acid.",
            "Lewis: acids accept an electron pair, bases donate one, forming a dative bond. This includes complex formation and BF3 with NH3.",
            r"$\text{pH} = -\log[\mathrm{H^+}]$ and $K_w = [\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{OH^-}] = 1.00\times10^{-14}$ at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$, so $\text{pH} + \text{pOH} = 14$.",
            "For strong acids and bases the ionisation is complete. Count the OH- ions in the formula of the base.",
            "For dilution find the new concentration; for mixing find the excess moles and divide by the total volume."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. Ka, Kb and Kw
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Ka, Kb and Kw",
    title="Weak acids and bases: Ka, Kb and pKa",
    slug="chu1-ka-kb-kw",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Write Ka and Kb expressions, calculate the pH of weak acids and bases using standard approximations, find Ka from pH data, and use Ka x Kb = Kw for conjugate pairs.",
    objectives=[
        "Write expressions for Ka and Kb and define pKa and pKb",
        "Calculate the pH of a weak acid or weak base using [H+] = sqrt(Ka c) and the equivalent for bases",
        "Find Ka from the pH of a solution of known concentration",
        "Use Ka x Kb = Kw to link an acid with its conjugate base, and explain how dilution affects the degree of ionisation",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Weak acid", "Ka and pKa", "Kb and pKb", "Approximations", "Degree of ionisation", "Conjugate pair constants"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Vinegar contains ethanoic acid. A solution of the same concentration of hydrochloric acid would be much more corrosive. Both are acids, yet the hydrochloric acid has the lower pH. The difference is that HCl ionises **completely** and ethanoic acid only **slightly**. To put numbers on this we use an equilibrium constant."),
        H("Ka for a weak acid"),
        T(r"A weak acid HA ionises partly in water. At equilibrium most of the acid is still in the form HA:"),
        F(r"\mathrm{HA(aq)} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+(aq)} + \mathrm{A^-(aq)}"),
        D(r"The **acid dissociation constant** is", title="Ka and pKa",
          formula=r"K_a = \frac{[\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}, \qquad \mathrm{p}K_a = -\log_{10}K_a",
          note="Units of Ka: mol per dm3. The bigger the Ka (the smaller the pKa), the stronger the acid. Water is not in the expression because its concentration is constant."),
        T(r"For ethanoic acid at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$, $K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$). Values of $K_a$ in textbooks differ slightly (some give $1.7\times10^{-5}$ or $1.74\times10^{-5}$), so check your textbook; every question will state the value to use."),
        H("The pH of a weak acid: two approximations"),
        T(r"Let the starting concentration of HA be $c$. Two approximations make the calculation simple:"),
        KEY("The two approximations", "State them in your answer.", cards=[
            [r"1. $[\mathrm{H^+}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$", "The acid is the only important source of H+. We ignore the tiny amount from water."],
            [r"2. $[\mathrm{HA}] \approx c$", "Very little of the acid has ionised, so the equilibrium concentration of HA is almost the starting value. This is valid if less than about 5% ionises."],
        ]),
        F(r"K_a = \frac{[\mathrm{H^+}]^2}{c} \quad\Rightarrow\quad [\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a\,c}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: pH of ethanoic acid", intro=r"Calculate the pH of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$) and the percentage that is ionised.", steps=[
            S("Write the expression and apply the approximations", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a\,c} = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-5} \times 0.100}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-6}} = 1.34\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(1.34\times10^{-3}) = 2.87"),
            S("Percentage ionised", math=r"\frac{1.34\times10^{-3}}{0.100} \times 100 = 1.3\%"),
            S("Check the approximation", text="1.3% is below 5%, so the approximation is valid."),
        ], check=r"\frac{(1.34\times10^{-3})^2}{0.100} = 1.8\times10^{-5}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: finding Ka from the pH", intro=r"A $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ solution of a weak acid HA has pH $3.20$. Calculate $K_a$ and $\mathrm{p}K_a$.", steps=[
            S("Hydrogen ion concentration", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = 10^{-3.20} = 6.31\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Apply $[\\mathrm{A^-}] = [\\mathrm{H^+}]$ and $[\\mathrm{HA}] \\approx 0.0500$", math=r"K_a = \frac{(6.31\times10^{-4})^2}{0.0500}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"K_a = 7.96\times10^{-6} \approx 8.0\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pKa", math=r"\mathrm{p}K_a = -\log_{10}(7.96\times10^{-6}) = 5.10"),
        ]),
        H("Weak bases, Kb and conjugate pairs"),
        D(r"For a weak base B in water, $\mathrm{B} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{BH^+} + \mathrm{OH^-}$:", title="Kb and pKb",
          formula=r"K_b = \frac{[\mathrm{BH^+}][\mathrm{OH^-}]}{[\mathrm{B}]}, \qquad [\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b\,c}",
          note="The same two approximations apply. Then find pOH and use pH = 14 - pOH at 25 degrees C."),
        D(r"For a conjugate acid-base pair such as $\mathrm{NH_4^+/NH_3}$, multiply the two constants:", title="Link between Ka, Kb and Kw",
          formula=r"K_a \times K_b = K_w \qquad\text{and}\qquad \mathrm{p}K_a + \mathrm{p}K_b = 14 \ (25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C})",
          note="A strong acid has a very weak conjugate base, and a weak acid has a weak-but-not-negligible conjugate base."),
        EX("Worked example 3: ammonia and its conjugate acid", intro=r"Ammonia has $K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. (a) Calculate the pH of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonia solution. (b) Calculate $K_a$ and $\mathrm{p}K_a$ of the ammonium ion. ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$)", steps=[
            S("(a) Hydroxide ion concentration", math=r"[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b\,c} = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-5}\times0.200} = 1.90\times10^{-3}"),
            S("pOH", math=r"\text{pOH} = -\log_{10}(1.90\times10^{-3}) = 2.72"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = 14.00 - 2.72 = 11.28"),
            S("(b) Ka of ammonium", math=r"K_a = \frac{K_w}{K_b} = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{1.8\times10^{-5}} = 5.6\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pKa", math=r"\mathrm{p}K_a = 14.00 - 4.74 = 9.26"),
        ]),
        T(r"Since $[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a c}$, the fraction ionised is $\sqrt{K_a c}/c = \sqrt{K_a/c}$. This **rises** as $c$ falls. For $0.0100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid, $[\mathrm{H^+}] = 4.2\times10^{-4}$ and the percentage ionised is 4.2%, compared with 1.3% at $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. A weak acid becomes *more* ionised when diluted, although the concentration of $\mathrm{H^+}$ itself falls. At very low concentrations the approximation $[\mathrm{HA}] \approx c$ is no longer valid."),
        MISTAKE("Treating a weak acid like a strong acid", r"For a weak acid $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ is **not** equal to the acid concentration. Use $\sqrt{K_a c}$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the Ka formula for a base", r"For a weak base the formula gives $[\mathrm{OH^-}]$, not $[\mathrm{H^+}]$. Find pOH first, then pH."),
        QUIZ(r"Four acids (invented) have $\mathrm{p}K_a$ values: P 4.2, Q 2.9, R 6.5, S 9.1. Which is the strongest acid?",
             ["P", "Q", "R", "S"], 1, r"The strongest acid has the smallest $\mathrm{p}K_a$, so the largest $K_a$: acid Q.",
             feedback=["Acid P has a larger pKa than Q, so a smaller Ka.", "", "R is weaker than P and Q.", "S is the weakest, with the largest pKa."], hint="A bigger Ka means a smaller pKa."),
        QUIZ(r"The $K_b$ of a base is $1.0\times10^{-5}$. What is the $K_a$ of its conjugate acid? ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$)",
             [r"$1.0\times10^{-9}$", r"$1.0\times10^{-19}$", r"$1.0\times10^{-5}$", r"$1.0\times10^{9}$"], 0, r"$K_a = K_w / K_b = 10^{-14} / 10^{-5} = 1.0\times10^{-9}$.",
             feedback=["", "You multiplied Kw by Kb.", "Ka is not equal to Kb.", "You divided Kb by Kw."], hint=r"Use $K_a K_b = K_w$."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of $0.020\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$). State your approximations.",
           r"Assume $[\mathrm{H^+}] = [\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}]$ and $[\mathrm{CH_3COOH}] \approx 0.020$. $[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-5}\times0.020} = 6.0\times10^{-4}$. $\text{pH} = -\log(6.0\times10^{-4}) = 3.22$. The percentage ionised is $3.0\%$, so the approximation is valid."),
        PQ(r"A $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ solution of a weak monoprotic acid has pH $3.20$. Calculate $K_a$ and explain why you can say $[\mathrm{HA}] \approx 0.0500$.",
           r"$[\mathrm{H^+}] = 10^{-3.20} = 6.31\times10^{-4}$. $K_a = \frac{(6.31\times10^{-4})^2}{0.0500} = 8.0\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. The amount ionised, $6.31\times10^{-4}$, is about 1.3% of $0.0500$, so the equilibrium concentration of HA is almost unchanged."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonia ($K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}$). Then calculate the $\mathrm{p}K_a$ of the ammonium ion.",
           r"$[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-5}\times0.200} = 1.90\times10^{-3}$, $\text{pOH} = 2.72$, $\text{pH} = 11.28$. $\mathrm{p}K_b = 4.74$, so $\mathrm{p}K_a = 14.00 - 4.74 = 9.26$."),
        PQ("Explain why the percentage ionisation of a weak acid increases when the solution is diluted, even though the pH rises.",
           r"Ka is constant. When the solution is diluted, the equilibrium $\mathrm{HA} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{A^-}$ shifts to the side with more particles to restore the value of the Ka expression, so a larger fraction of HA ionises. The concentration of $\mathrm{H^+}$ still falls (because the total acid is more dilute), so the pH rises."),
        TIP("In the exam: weak acid and base pH calculations, finding Ka from pH and the Ka x Kb = Kw link are central to Paper 2 (and a common source of Paper 1 calculation items). State your approximations and the value of Kw or Ka that you use."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Weak acids ionise partly: $K_a = [\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}]$; $\mathrm{p}K_a = -\log K_a$. A smaller pKa means a stronger acid.",
            r"Assuming $[\mathrm{H^+}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$ and $[\mathrm{HA}] \approx c$: $[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a c}$. For a weak base $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b c}$.",
            r"For a conjugate pair, $K_a K_b = K_w$ and $\mathrm{p}K_a + \mathrm{p}K_b = 14$ at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$.",
            r"Find $K_a$ from the pH using $K_a = [\mathrm{H^+}]^2/c$.",
            "Dilution increases the percentage ionised of a weak acid."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9. Buffer solutions: how they work
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Buffer solutions",
    title="Buffer solutions: how they work",
    slug="chu1-buffers-how-they-work",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Define a buffer, describe acidic and basic buffers, explain how they resist pH change when small amounts of acid or base are added, and calculate the pH from Ka and the ratio of acid to salt.",
    objectives=[
        "Define a buffer solution and give an acidic and a basic example",
        "Explain with equations how a buffer resists change when H+ or OH- is added",
        "Make a buffer by mixing a weak acid with its salt, or by partly neutralising a weak acid",
        "Calculate the pH of a buffer using Ka = [H+][A-]/[HA]",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Buffer solution", "Acidic and basic buffer", "Reservoir of weak acid and conjugate base", "Partial neutralisation", "Buffer capacity", "Blood as a buffer"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"The pH of your blood must stay between 7.35 and 7.45. Outside this range you become seriously ill. Yet you eat acidic foods such as tomatoes and oranges every day, and your cells produce carbon dioxide and lactic acid. Something prevents your blood pH from changing much. That something is a **buffer**."),
        D(r"A **buffer solution** is a solution whose pH changes very little when small amounts of acid or base are added, or when it is diluted slightly.", title="Buffer solution"),
        T(r"A buffer needs two things at once: a species that can **remove added H+ ions**, and a species that can **remove added OH- ions**. Together they must not neutralise each other. A weak acid and its conjugate base do this."),
        KEY("Two kinds of buffer", "Both have a large amount of each component.", cards=[
            ["Acidic buffer", r"A weak acid and its salt, for example $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ with $\mathrm{CH_3COONa}$. pH below 7."],
            ["Basic buffer", r"A weak base and its salt, for example $\mathrm{NH_3}$ with $\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$. pH above 7."],
        ]),
        T(r"In an ethanoic acid / sodium ethanoate buffer, the acid is only slightly ionised and the salt is fully ionised. So the solution contains a large amount of undissociated $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ and a large amount of ethanoate ions $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$ (from the salt):"),
        F(r"\mathrm{CH_3COOH} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{CH_3COO^-}"),
        H("How a buffer resists change"),
        T(r"""**Adding a little acid ($\mathrm{H^+}$).** The extra $\mathrm{H^+}$ combines with the ethanoate ions, which are in large supply: $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COOH}$. The equilibrium shifts to the left, and $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ stays almost the same.

**Adding a little base ($\mathrm{OH^-}$).** The extra $\mathrm{OH^-}$ reacts with $\mathrm{H^+}$ to form water. This lowers $[\mathrm{H^+}]$, so more ethanoic acid ionises to replace it: $\mathrm{CH_3COOH} + \mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H_2O}$. The acid is the supply of $\mathrm{H^+}$.

In each case, only a small *fraction* of one component changes, because both are present in large amounts. The pH depends on the **ratio** $[\mathrm{HA}]/[\mathrm{A^-}]$, which hardly changes."""),
        H("The pH of a buffer"),
        T(r"Start from the expression for $K_a$ and make $[\mathrm{H^+}]$ the subject:"),
        F(r"K_a = \frac{[\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]} \quad\Rightarrow\quad [\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a\,\frac{[\mathrm{HA}]}{[\mathrm{A^-}]}"),
        T(r"We use the starting concentrations of the acid and the salt for $[\mathrm{HA}]$ and $[\mathrm{A^-}]$. This is valid because the salt is fully ionised, which suppresses the ionisation of the acid (the common ion effect), so very little acid has ionised."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a buffer from two solutions", intro=r"A solution is $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ in ethanoic acid and $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ in sodium ethanoate. ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$.) Calculate the pH. Then repeat for $0.100$ and $0.100$.", steps=[
            S("Use the Ka expression", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a\,\frac{[\mathrm{HA}]}{[\mathrm{A^-}]} = 1.8\times10^{-5} \times \frac{0.200}{0.100}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = 3.6\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(3.6\times10^{-5}) = 4.44"),
            S("Equal concentrations", text=r"If $[\mathrm{HA}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$ the ratio is 1, so $[\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a$ and $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$."),
        ]),
        H("Making a buffer by partial neutralisation"),
        T(r"You do not need to buy the salt. Add less than enough strong base to a weak acid. Some of the acid is converted to its salt, giving a mixture of both. The amounts must be worked out in **moles**."),
        EX("Worked example 2: partial neutralisation", intro=r"$50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid is mixed with $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$.) Calculate the pH.", steps=[
            S("Moles of acid and alkali", math=r"n(\mathrm{HA}) = 5.00\times10^{-3}, \qquad n(\mathrm{OH^-}) = 2.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("The alkali converts HA to $\\mathrm{A^-}$ 1 : 1", math=r"n(\mathrm{A^-}) = 2.00\times10^{-3}, \qquad n(\mathrm{HA})_{\text{left}} = 3.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Both are in the same volume, so use the mole ratio", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.8\times10^{-5} \times \frac{3.00\times10^{-3}}{2.00\times10^{-3}} = 2.7\times10^{-5}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(2.7\times10^{-5}) = 4.57"),
        ], check=r"\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a + \log\frac{2.00}{3.00} = 4.74 - 0.18 = 4.57"),
        H("Buffer capacity, and buffers in the body"),
        T(r"""A buffer cannot take unlimited acid or base. When one component has been used up, the pH changes quickly. The **buffer capacity** is greater when the concentrations of the acid and salt are larger, and is greatest when they are about equal, because then the buffer can absorb acid and base equally. A buffer works best within about one pH unit of the $\mathrm{p}K_a$ of the weak acid.

Blood is buffered mainly by carbonic acid and hydrogencarbonate ions: $\mathrm{CO_2} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H_2CO_3} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{HCO_3^-}$. Extra $\mathrm{H^+}$ is removed by $\mathrm{HCO_3^-}$; extra base is removed as the acid supplies $\mathrm{H^+}$. The lungs adjust the carbon dioxide, and the kidneys adjust the hydrogencarbonate. In laboratories buffers are used to calibrate pH meters and to keep the pH fixed for enzyme or titration work."""),
        MISTAKE("Mixing equal amounts of acid and alkali", r"If you add exactly enough NaOH to react with all the weak acid, you have only the salt, not a buffer. Leave some acid unreacted."),
        QUIZ(r"Which mixture is an acidic buffer?",
             [r"$25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl and $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH", r"$25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl and $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaCl", r"$50\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ and $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH", r"$50\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH and $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaCl"], 2,
             r"Half of the weak acid is neutralised, giving equal amounts of $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ and $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$.",
             feedback=["These neutralise exactly, giving a neutral salt solution with no weak acid.", "HCl is a strong acid and NaCl has no conjugate base that can remove H+.", "", "There is no weak acid or weak base present."], hint="A buffer needs a weak acid and its conjugate base."),
        QUIZ(r"A small amount of $\mathrm{OH^-}$ is added to an ethanoic acid / ethanoate buffer. What happens?",
             [r"$\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$ reacts with the $\mathrm{OH^-}$", r"$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ is used up completely", r"$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ reacts with the $\mathrm{OH^-}$ and the pH stays almost constant", r"The pH rises sharply"], 2,
             r"$\mathrm{CH_3COOH} + \mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H_2O}$: only a small fraction of the acid is used.",
             feedback=["Ethanoate is a weak base; it removes H+, not OH-.", "Only a small amount reacts; the acid is in large supply.", "", "That would happen without the buffer."], hint="Which component reacts with a base?"),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Explain, using equations, how a mixture of ammonia and ammonium chloride acts as a buffer.",
           r"Ammonium chloride is fully ionised, so $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ is in large supply. Ammonia is a weak base and is present in large amount. Added acid: $\mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{NH_4^+}$. Added alkali: $\mathrm{NH_4^+} + \mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H_2O}$. The ratio $[\mathrm{NH_4^+}]/[\mathrm{NH_3}]$, and so the pH, hardly changes."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of a solution that contains $0.150\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid and $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ sodium ethanoate ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$).",
           r"$[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.8\times10^{-5}\times\frac{0.150}{0.0500} = 5.4\times10^{-5}$, so $\text{pH} = -\log(5.4\times10^{-5}) = 4.27$."),
        PQ(r"Describe how you would prepare a buffer from ethanoic acid and sodium hydroxide only, and say why the quantities matter.",
           r"Take a known volume and concentration of ethanoic acid and add a smaller known amount of NaOH. For example, add enough NaOH to react with half of the acid, which gives equal amounts of ethanoic acid and ethanoate and a pH equal to the $\mathrm{p}K_a$. If you add too much NaOH, no weak acid is left; if you add too little, there is too little salt. In both cases it will not buffer both ways."),
        PQ(r"Why is the buffer in blood important, and which conjugate pair provides most of it?",
           r"Blood pH must stay between about 7.35 and 7.45, because enzymes and cells work only in a narrow pH range. Most of the buffering comes from carbonic acid and hydrogencarbonate ions, $\mathrm{H_2CO_3/HCO_3^-}$, which remove added $\mathrm{H^+}$ or $\mathrm{OH^-}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: how a buffer works, the choice of mixture, and pH of a buffer are Paper 2 items (usually 5 to 8 marks in a multi-part question) and Paper 1 items. Always use equations to show the response to added acid and to added base."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A buffer resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added. It contains a weak acid and its conjugate base (or a weak base and its conjugate acid) in large amounts.",
            r"Added $\mathrm{H^+}$ is removed by the conjugate base; added $\mathrm{OH^-}$ is removed by the weak acid.",
            r"$[\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a[\mathrm{HA}]/[\mathrm{A^-}]$. When the two are equal, $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a$.",
            "A buffer can be made by partial neutralisation of a weak acid. Work in moles.",
            "Capacity is greater with higher concentrations and is greatest when [HA] and [A-] are equal."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10. Buffer calculations
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Buffer solutions",
    title="Buffer calculations and the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation",
    slug="chu1-buffer-calculations",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]) to find the pH of buffers, the pH change when small amounts of acid or base are added, and the amounts needed to prepare a buffer of chosen pH.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive and use the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for acidic and basic buffers",
        "Calculate the pH after adding strong acid or base to a buffer, using moles",
        "Calculate the quantities needed to make a buffer of a required pH",
        "Choose a suitable weak acid for a target pH",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Henderson-Hasselbalch equation", "pH = pKa + log ratio", "Mole method for added acid or base", "Designing a buffer", "Basic buffer"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A technician in a Douala laboratory is asked to prepare $500\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a buffer at pH 5.00 to keep an enzyme reaction stable. She cannot guess the quantities. She uses an equation that links the pH of a buffer directly to the ratio of its two components."),
        H("The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation"),
        T(r"In the previous lesson we found $[\mathrm{H^+}] = K_a\,[\mathrm{HA}]/[\mathrm{A^-}]$. Take $-\log_{10}$ of both sides. Since $-\log(ab) = -\log a - \log b$:"),
        F(r"-\log[\mathrm{H^+}] = -\log K_a - \log\frac{[\mathrm{HA}]}{[\mathrm{A^-}]}"),
        D(r"Using $-\log\frac{[\mathrm{HA}]}{[\mathrm{A^-}]} = +\log\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}$ we get the **Henderson-Hasselbalch equation**:", title="Henderson-Hasselbalch equation",
          formula=r"\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a + \log_{10}\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}",
          note="A^- is the conjugate base (the salt) and HA the weak acid. If both are in the same volume you can use moles instead of concentrations. Equal amounts give pH = pKa."),
        T(r"Each tenfold increase in the ratio $[\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}]$ raises the pH by 1. For a **basic buffer** use the same idea with $\mathrm{p}K_b$: $\text{pOH} = \mathrm{p}K_b + \log\frac{[\mathrm{BH^+}]}{[\mathrm{B}]}$, then $\text{pH} = 14 - \text{pOH}$ at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: pH from a mixture", intro=r"$50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid is mixed with $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.745$.) Find the pH.", steps=[
            S("Moles", math=r"n(\mathrm{HA}) = 0.200\times0.0500 = 1.00\times10^{-2}, \qquad n(\mathrm{OH^-}) = 0.100\times0.0250 = 2.50\times10^{-3}"),
            S("After reaction", math=r"n(\mathrm{A^-}) = 2.50\times10^{-3}, \qquad n(\mathrm{HA}) = 7.50\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Henderson-Hasselbalch (moles are fine: the volume cancels)", math=r"\text{pH} = 4.745 + \log\frac{2.50\times10^{-3}}{7.50\times10^{-3}} = 4.745 + \log 0.3333"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"\text{pH} = 4.745 - 0.477 = 4.268 \approx 4.27"),
        ]),
        H("How well does the buffer resist? Adding acid"),
        EX("Worked example 2: adding acid to a buffer, and to water", intro=r"A buffer is $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of solution containing $0.100\ \mathrm{mol}$ ethanoic acid and $0.100\ \mathrm{mol}$ sodium ethanoate ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$). $0.0010\ \mathrm{mol}$ of HCl is added (no volume change). Find the new pH and compare with adding the same HCl to $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of pure water.", steps=[
            S("The added H+ reacts with ethanoate", math=r"\mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COOH}"),
            S("New moles", math=r"n(\mathrm{A^-}) = 0.100 - 0.0010 = 0.0990, \qquad n(\mathrm{HA}) = 0.100 + 0.0010 = 0.1010"),
            S("New pH", math=r"\text{pH} = 4.745 + \log\frac{0.0990}{0.1010} = 4.745 - 0.009 = 4.736"),
            S("Change", math=r"\Delta\text{pH} = -0.009"),
            S("Same acid in pure water", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = 1.0\times10^{-3}\ \Rightarrow\ \text{pH} = 3.00\ \ (\text{a fall of } 4.00 \text{ from pH } 7)"),
        ], check=r"\log\frac{0.0990}{0.1010} = \log(0.9802) = -0.00867"),
        T(r"The buffer changed by less than 0.01 pH unit. Pure water changed by 4 pH units. This is the buffering effect, in numbers."),
        H("Designing a buffer of chosen pH"),
        T(r"Rearrange the equation to find the ratio you need, then the amounts. For a good buffer choose a weak acid with $\mathrm{p}K_a$ within about 1 unit of the target pH, so that the ratio is between 0.1 and 10."),
        EX("Worked example 3: preparing a buffer at pH 5.00", intro=r"What mass of sodium ethanoate ($M_r = 82.0$) must be dissolved in $500\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid to give pH $5.00$? ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$ using $K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$; assume no volume change.)", steps=[
            S("Rearrange the equation", math=r"\log\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]} = \text{pH} - \mathrm{p}K_a = 5.00 - 4.745 = 0.255"),
            S("The ratio", math=r"\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]} = 10^{0.255} = 1.80"),
            S("Concentration and moles of ethanoate needed", math=r"[\mathrm{A^-}] = 1.80\times0.100 = 0.180\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}, \qquad n = 0.180\times0.500 = 0.0900\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Mass", math=r"m = 0.0900 \times 82.0 = 7.38\ \mathrm{g}"),
        ], check=r"\log\frac{0.180}{0.100} = 0.255,\ \ 4.745 + 0.255 = 5.00"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a basic buffer", intro=r"A solution contains $0.150\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonia and $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonium chloride. $K_b(\mathrm{NH_3}) = 1.8\times10^{-5}$. Find the pH.", steps=[
            S("pKb", math=r"\mathrm{p}K_b = -\log(1.8\times10^{-5}) = 4.745"),
            S("pOH", math=r"\text{pOH} = 4.745 + \log\frac{0.100}{0.150} = 4.745 - 0.176 = 4.569"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = 14.00 - 4.569 = 9.43"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Putting the ratio upside down", r"It is $[\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}]$ (salt over acid) with $+\log$. A quick check: more salt than acid must give a pH **above** $\mathrm{p}K_a$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the added acid in the wrong direction", r"Added strong acid turns $\mathrm{A^-}$ into HA, so the ratio falls and the pH falls. Added alkali does the opposite. Adjust the moles of both."),
        MISTAKE("Using moles of the buffer after dilution and forgetting that moles are enough", r"Because $[\mathrm{A^-}]$ and $[\mathrm{HA}]$ share the same volume, the volume cancels in the ratio. You may use moles directly; you need the volume only if concentrations are asked for."),
        QUIZ(r"A buffer has $[\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}] = 10$ and $\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$. What is its pH?",
             ["3.74", "4.74", "5.74", "14.74"], 2, r"$\text{pH} = 4.74 + \log 10 = 5.74$.",
             feedback=["That is pKa minus 1, from an inverted ratio.", "That would need a ratio of 1.", "", "You added 10 instead of log 10."], hint=r"$\log 10 = 1$."),
        QUIZ(r"For a target pH of 9.0, which weak acid (with $\mathrm{p}K_a$ given) would be the best choice to make a buffer?",
             [r"Acid A, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.8$", r"Acid B, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 9.3$", r"Acid C, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 2.9$", r"Acid D, $\mathrm{p}K_a = 6.4$"], 1,
             r"The best buffer has $\mathrm{p}K_a$ close to the target pH, so the ratio is near 1. Acid B is within 0.3 of 9.0.",
             feedback=["The ratio would need to be very small (about 10 to the minus 4).", "", "The ratio would need to be about 10 to the 6.", "The ratio would be about 1 to 400, too far from 1."], hint=r"Look for $\mathrm{p}K_a$ near the target pH."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of the buffer made by adding $0.0010\ \mathrm{mol}$ NaOH to $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of the buffer in Worked example 2 ($0.100\ \mathrm{mol}$ each; $\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.745$).",
           r"$\mathrm{CH_3COOH} + \mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H_2O}$, so $n(\mathrm{HA}) = 0.0990$ and $n(\mathrm{A^-}) = 0.1010$. $\text{pH} = 4.745 + \log\frac{0.1010}{0.0990} = 4.745 + 0.009 = 4.754$, about 4.75."),
        PQ(r"A student needs a buffer of pH 4.30 using methanoic acid ($\mathrm{p}K_a = 3.75$, invented simplified value). Calculate the ratio of $[\mathrm{HCOO^-}]$ to $[\mathrm{HCOOH}]$.",
           r"$\log\frac{[\mathrm{A^-}]}{[\mathrm{HA}]} = 4.30 - 3.75 = 0.55$, so the ratio is $10^{0.55} = 3.55$."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of a solution made by mixing $100\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.20\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonia with $50\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.20\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl. ($K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}$)",
           r"$n(\mathrm{NH_3}) = 0.020$, $n(\mathrm{HCl}) = 0.010$. The HCl converts $0.010$ mol of ammonia to ammonium, leaving $0.010$ mol $\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $0.010$ mol $\mathrm{NH_4^+}$. The ratio is 1, so $\text{pOH} = \mathrm{p}K_b = 4.745$ and $\text{pH} = 14.00 - 4.745 = 9.26$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a buffer containing $1.0\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ of each component is better able to resist added acid than one containing $0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ of each, though both have the same pH.",
           r"Both have the ratio 1 and the same pH. But the more concentrated buffer has a much larger amount of $\mathrm{A^-}$ available to react with added $\mathrm{H^+}$. The fraction of $\mathrm{A^-}$ that is used up, and so the change in the ratio, is smaller. The buffer capacity is greater."),
        TIP("In the exam: Henderson-Hasselbalch calculations with a mixture of moles, a change after adding acid or base, and a design question are typical Paper 2 items. In Paper 1, quick ratio calculations appear. Always state the Ka or pKa you use."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a + \log([\mathrm{A^-}]/[\mathrm{HA}])$. Equal amounts give $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a$; a tenfold ratio changes the pH by 1.",
            "Moles can replace concentrations in the ratio because the volume cancels.",
            "Added acid converts A- to HA (pH falls slightly); added alkali converts HA to A- (pH rises slightly). Work out the new moles first.",
            r"To design a buffer, find the ratio from $10^{\text{pH} - \mathrm{p}K_a}$ and choose an acid with $\mathrm{p}K_a$ within one unit of the target pH.",
            r"For a basic buffer use $\text{pOH} = \mathrm{p}K_b + \log([\mathrm{BH^+}]/[\mathrm{B}])$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 11. Titration curves and indicators
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Titration curves and indicators",
    title="Titration curves and the choice of indicator",
    slug="chu1-titration-curves-indicators",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Sketch and interpret pH curves for strong and weak acids and bases, calculate key points, explain how an indicator changes colour, and choose an indicator from the steep part of the curve.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe and sketch titration curves for strong acid-strong base and weak acid-strong base",
        "Calculate the pH at the start, at half-equivalence and at the equivalence point",
        "Explain how a weak-acid indicator works and why its pH range is about pKin plus or minus 1",
        "Choose a suitable indicator for each type of titration and find pKa from a curve",
    ],
    key_concepts=["pH curve", "Equivalence point", "End point", "Half-equivalence", "Indicator", "pKin and range"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In a school laboratory you titrate hydrochloric acid with sodium hydroxide, adding a few drops of phenolphthalein. The colour changes from colourless to pink at the right moment. But why phenolphthalein? Why not methyl orange? The answer lies in how the pH changes during the titration, which we can plot as a **pH curve**."),
        D(r"The **equivalence point** is where the amount of titrant added exactly reacts with the amount of substance in the flask, in the ratio of the equation. The **end point** is where the indicator changes colour. A good choice of indicator makes them agree.", title="Equivalence point and end point"),
        H("Strong acid with strong base"),
        T(r"The curve below is for $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ acid titrated with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH (data computed for this lesson). The curve for HCl starts at pH 1.00, rises slowly, then climbs almost vertically around $25\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ and levels off near pH 12.5."),
        IMG("al-chu1-titration-curves.svg", alt="Titration curves of 25 cm3 of 0.100 mol per dm3 acid with NaOH. Strong acid starts at pH 1, jumps from about 3.7 to 10.3 near 25 cm3. Weak acid starts near 2.9, rises through a gently sloping buffer region and jumps from about 7 to 10.3. Indicator ranges are shaded.", caption="Figure 1. pH curves for a strong acid (blue) and a weak acid of Ka = 1.8e-5 (brown) with strong base. The shaded bands are the ranges of methyl orange and phenolphthalein."),
        EX("Worked example 1: points on a strong acid-strong base curve", intro=r"Calculate the pH of $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl after adding (a) $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ and (b) $25.1\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$)", steps=[
            S("(a) Moles", math=r"n(\mathrm{H^+}) = 2.50\times10^{-3}, \qquad n(\mathrm{OH^-}) = 2.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Excess acid in $45.0\\ \\mathrm{cm^3}$", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \frac{5.0\times10^{-4}}{0.0450} = 0.0111 \Rightarrow \text{pH} = 1.95"),
            S("(b) Excess alkali in $50.1\\ \\mathrm{cm^3}$", math=r"n(\mathrm{OH^-})_{\text{excess}} = 1.0\times10^{-5},\quad [\mathrm{OH^-}] = \frac{1.0\times10^{-5}}{0.0501} = 2.0\times10^{-4}"),
            S("pOH, then pH", math=r"\text{pOH} = 3.70 \Rightarrow \text{pH} = 10.30"),
            S("Meaning", text="Only 0.2 cm3 (about 4 drops) takes the pH from about 3.7 (at 24.9 cm3) to 10.3. That is the steep part of the curve. The equivalence point at 25.0 cm3 has pH 7.00."),
        ]),
        H("Weak acid with strong base"),
        T(r"For a weak acid the curve is different in four ways. **Start:** a higher pH (about 2.9 for $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid). **Buffer region:** as NaOH is added, the weak acid and its salt both exist, so the pH changes slowly. **Half-equivalence:** half of the acid has been neutralised, so $[\mathrm{HA}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$ and $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a$. **Equivalence point:** the flask contains the salt of a weak acid, whose anion is a weak base, so the pH is **above 7**."),
        EX("Worked example 2: ethanoic acid with NaOH", intro=r"$25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$) is titrated with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Find the pH at (a) half-equivalence and (b) the equivalence point.", steps=[
            S("(a) Half-equivalence is at $12.5\\ \\mathrm{cm^3}$", math=r"[\mathrm{HA}] = [\mathrm{A^-}] \Rightarrow \text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74"),
            S("(b) At $25.0\\ \\mathrm{cm^3}$ the solution is sodium ethanoate", math=r"[\mathrm{A^-}] = \frac{2.50\times10^{-3}}{0.0500} = 0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("The ethanoate ion is a weak base", math=r"K_b = \frac{K_w}{K_a} = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{1.8\times10^{-5}} = 5.6\times10^{-10}"),
            S("Hydroxide ion concentration", math=r"[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b\,c} = \sqrt{5.6\times10^{-10}\times0.0500} = 5.3\times10^{-6}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pOH} = 5.28 \Rightarrow \text{pH} = 8.72"),
        ]),
        T(r"After the equivalence point the pH is controlled by the excess NaOH, so the weak and strong acid curves are identical there. The steep part for the weak acid is shorter, from about pH 7 to 10. For a **strong acid with a weak base** (for example HCl with ammonia) the equivalence point is below 7, because the salt $\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$ is acidic. For a **weak acid with a weak base** there is no sharp rise, so no indicator gives a clear end point."),
        H("Indicators"),
        D(r"An **indicator** is a weak acid HIn whose acid form and conjugate base form have different colours: $\mathrm{HIn} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{In^-}$, with $K_{in} = \dfrac{[\mathrm{H^+}][\mathrm{In^-}]}{[\mathrm{HIn}]}$.", title="Indicator"),
        T(r"In acid, the equilibrium is pushed to the left and we see the colour of HIn. In alkali, it is pushed to the right and we see the colour of $\mathrm{In^-}$. The colour appears to change when the two forms are present in comparable amounts. At the midpoint $[\mathrm{HIn}] = [\mathrm{In^-}]$, so $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_{in}$. The eye detects a change over a range of roughly one pH unit either side, so the **range** is about $\mathrm{p}K_{in} \pm 1$."),
        CODE("""Indicator          pH range     Colour in acid -> alkali
Methyl orange      3.1 - 4.4    red -> yellow
Methyl red         4.4 - 6.2    red -> yellow
Bromothymol blue   6.0 - 7.6    yellow -> blue
Phenolphthalein    8.3 - 10.0   colourless -> pink""", "text", title="Common indicators (check your textbook for exact ranges)"),
        KEY("Choosing an indicator", "The whole range of the indicator must lie on the steep part of the curve.", cards=[
            ["Strong acid, strong base", "Any of the indicators above: the steep part is very long (pH 3.7 to 10.3)."],
            ["Weak acid, strong base", "Phenolphthalein. Methyl orange would change colour too early, in the buffer region."],
            ["Strong acid, weak base", "Methyl orange or methyl red. Phenolphthalein would change colour before the steep part."],
            ["Weak acid, weak base", "None is suitable: there is no sharp change."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: pKa from a curve and the right indicator", intro=r"In a titration of $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a weak acid with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH (teaching data), the steep rise is centred on $24.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ and the pH after $12.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ is $4.80$. Find the concentration of the acid, its $K_a$, and suggest an indicator.", steps=[
            S("Moles of NaOH at equivalence", math=r"0.100\times0.0240 = 2.40\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Concentration of the monoprotic acid", math=r"c = \frac{2.40\times10^{-3}}{0.0250} = 0.0960\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Half-equivalence volume", math=r"\tfrac12\times24.0 = 12.0\ \mathrm{cm^3} \Rightarrow \mathrm{p}K_a = 4.80"),
            S("Ka", math=r"K_a = 10^{-4.80} = 1.6\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Indicator", text="A weak acid with a strong base has an equivalence point above 7, so choose phenolphthalein."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying the equivalence point of every titration is pH 7", r"It is 7 only for a strong acid with a strong base. A weak acid gives a pH above 7, and a weak base a pH below 7."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the indicator changes at the equivalence point exactly", r"The colour changes over a pH range. A suitable indicator has its whole range inside the vertical part of the curve, so any error in volume is only a drop or two."),
        MISTAKE("Using the pH at the start to find pKa", r"pKa is read at **half-equivalence**, where $[\mathrm{HA}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$. The starting pH needs the concentration and the square root formula."),
        QUIZ(r"$25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid is titrated with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Which indicator is most suitable?",
             ["Methyl orange (3.1-4.4)", "Methyl red (4.4-6.2)", "Phenolphthalein (8.3-10.0)", "None: the pH at equivalence is exactly 7"], 2,
             r"The equivalence pH is about 8.7 and the steep part runs from about 7 to 10.3. Phenolphthalein's range lies in that part.",
             feedback=["Methyl orange changes while the solution is still a buffer.", "Methyl red also changes before the steep part.", "", "The ethanoate ion makes the equivalence point alkaline."], hint="Find the pH at equivalence first."),
        QUIZ(r"At the half-equivalence point of a weak acid titration, which statement is true?",
             [r"$\text{pH} = 7$", r"$\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a$", r"$[\mathrm{HA}] = 0$", r"$[\mathrm{A^-}] = 0$"], 1,
             r"Half the acid has been converted to its anion, so $[\mathrm{HA}] = [\mathrm{A^-}]$ and the log term in the buffer equation is zero.",
             feedback=["pH 7 would be at equivalence for a strong acid.", "", "Half of the acid is still there.", "Half of the acid has been converted to A-."], hint="Use the buffer equation."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Sketch (describe) the pH curve for the titration of $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid with $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH, marking the starting pH, the half-equivalence point, and the equivalence point. ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$)",
           r"The curve starts at pH about 2.9 (from $\sqrt{K_a c}$), rises gently through the buffer region, passes $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a = 4.74$ at $12.5\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ (half-equivalence), then rises steeply around $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ with the equivalence point at pH about 8.7, and levels off near pH 12.5."),
        PQ(r"Explain why the equivalence point of the titration of ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide is above pH 7.",
           r"At equivalence the flask contains sodium ethanoate. The ethanoate ion is the conjugate base of a weak acid, so it accepts protons from water: $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{CH_3COOH} + \mathrm{OH^-}$. This produces hydroxide ions, so the solution is alkaline."),
        PQ(r"Methyl orange is a weak acid with $\mathrm{p}K_{in} = 3.7$. Explain why it changes colour between about pH 3.1 and 4.4, and why it would not be suitable for the ethanoic acid titration.",
           r"In acid $\mathrm{HIn}$ (red) dominates, and in alkali $\mathrm{In^-}$ (yellow) dominates. At $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_{in} = 3.7$ the two are equal. The colour appears to change when one form is about ten times the other, which is at pH about $\mathrm{p}K_{in} \pm 1$. For ethanoic acid the pH is already above 4.4 during the buffer region, long before equivalence, so methyl orange would change colour too early."),
        PQ(r"$25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a weak monoprotic acid of unknown concentration needed $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH for neutralisation, and the pH after $10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of NaOH was 3.90. Calculate the concentration and $K_a$ of the acid.",
           r"$n(\mathrm{NaOH}) = 0.0500\times0.0200 = 1.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$, so $c = \frac{1.00\times10^{-3}}{0.0250} = 0.0400\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. $10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ is half of $20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$, so $\mathrm{p}K_a = 3.90$ and $K_a = 10^{-3.90} = 1.3\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: sketching pH curves, reading pKa from half-equivalence, calculating pH at the equivalence point and choosing an indicator appear in Paper 2. Paper 1 asks quick questions on the choice of indicator. The practical Paper 3 uses titrations, with phenolphthalein or methyl orange."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A pH curve plots pH against the volume of titrant. The steep part is centred on the equivalence point.",
            "Strong acid with strong base: starts low, equivalence pH 7, long steep part. Weak acid with strong base: starts higher, has a buffer region, equivalence pH above 7.",
            r"At half-equivalence of a weak acid, $\text{pH} = \mathrm{p}K_a$.",
            r"Indicator: $\mathrm{HIn} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H^+} + \mathrm{In^-}$, with a colour range of about $\mathrm{p}K_{in} \pm 1$.",
            "Choose an indicator whose whole range lies within the steep part of the curve. No indicator suits a weak acid with a weak base."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 12. Salt hydrolysis
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Salt hydrolysis",
    title="Salt hydrolysis: why some salts are acidic or alkaline",
    slug="chu1-salt-hydrolysis",
    minutes=21,
    summary="Predict whether a salt solution is acidic, alkaline or neutral, explain it with equations for hydrolysis, and calculate the pH of a salt of a weak acid or weak base.",
    objectives=[
        "Predict the acidity of the four types of salt solution",
        "Write equations for the hydrolysis of anions of weak acids, cations of weak bases and small highly charged metal ions",
        "Calculate the pH of a salt solution using Kw = Ka x Kb",
        "Use the pH of a salt solution to find the Ka of its parent acid",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Hydrolysis", "Conjugate base of a weak acid", "Conjugate acid of a weak base", "Metal ion hydrolysis", "Neutral, acidic and alkaline salts"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Washing soda (sodium carbonate) is used for laundry because its solution is alkaline and cuts grease. A solution of alum, used to clear muddy river water for drinking in some places, is acidic. Yet neither contains an acid or a base as we normally think of them. These are **salts**. The pH of a salt solution depends on what its ions do to water."),
        D(r"**Salt hydrolysis** is the reaction of an ion of a salt with water, which changes the concentration of $\mathrm{H^+}$ or $\mathrm{OH^-}$ in the solution.", title="Hydrolysis"),
        T(r"Think of a salt as the product of an acid and a base. The ions that come from a **strong** acid or base do not react with water (they are very weak conjugates). The ions that come from a **weak** acid or base do."),
        CODE("""Parent acid / base           Example salt     pH of solution (25 C)
Strong acid + strong base    NaCl             7 (neutral)
Strong acid + weak base      NH4Cl            below 7 (acidic)
Weak acid + strong base      CH3COONa         above 7 (alkaline)
Weak acid + weak base        CH3COONH4        near 7 if Ka is about Kb""", "text", title="Salt solutions"),
        T(r"The ethanoate ion is the conjugate base of a weak acid, so it accepts protons from water:"),
        F(r"\mathrm{CH_3COO^-} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{CH_3COOH} + \mathrm{OH^-}"),
        T(r"The equilibrium constant is $K_b$ of the ethanoate ion, found from $K_b = K_w / K_a$ with $K_a$ of the acid. The $\mathrm{OH^-}$ formed makes the solution alkaline. Carbonate behaves in the same way: $\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{HCO_3^-} + \mathrm{OH^-}$."),
        T(r"The ammonium ion is the conjugate acid of a weak base, so it donates protons to water:"),
        F(r"\mathrm{NH_4^+} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H_3O^+}"),
        T(r"Its $K_a$ is found from $K_a = K_w / K_b$ with $K_b$ of ammonia. The $\mathrm{H_3O^+}$ formed makes the solution acidic."),
        EX("Worked example 1: pH of ammonium chloride", intro=r"Calculate the pH of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ammonium chloride. ($K_b$ of ammonia $= 1.8\times10^{-5}$; $K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$.)", steps=[
            S("Chloride ions do not react. The ammonium ion is a weak acid", math=r"K_a(\mathrm{NH_4^+}) = \frac{K_w}{K_b} = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{1.8\times10^{-5}} = 5.6\times10^{-10}"),
            S("Weak acid approximation", math=r"[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a\,c} = \sqrt{5.6\times10^{-10}\times0.100} = 7.5\times10^{-6}"),
            S("pH", math=r"\text{pH} = -\log_{10}(7.5\times10^{-6}) = 5.13"),
        ], check=r"\text{percentage hydrolysed} = \frac{7.5\times10^{-6}}{0.100}\times100 = 0.0075\%\ (\text{the approximation is valid})"),
        EX("Worked example 2: pH of sodium ethanoate", intro=r"Calculate the pH of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ sodium ethanoate. ($K_a$ of ethanoic acid $= 1.8\times10^{-5}$.)", steps=[
            S("Sodium ions do not react. The ethanoate ion is a weak base", math=r"K_b = \frac{K_w}{K_a} = 5.6\times10^{-10}"),
            S("Weak base approximation", math=r"[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b\,c} = \sqrt{5.6\times10^{-10}\times0.100} = 7.5\times10^{-6}"),
            S("pOH, then pH", math=r"\text{pOH} = 5.13 \Rightarrow \text{pH} = 14.00 - 5.13 = 8.87"),
        ]),
        T(r"The two answers are mirror images (5.13 and 8.87 add to 14) because ethanoic acid and ammonia happen to have the same strength, $K_a = K_b = 1.8\times10^{-5}$. If you mix them to make **ammonium ethanoate**, each ion hydrolyses to the same extent, so the solution is nearly neutral (pH about 7)."),
        H("Metal ions: small, highly charged cations"),
        T(r"Transition metal ions and aluminium ions are surrounded by water molecules in solution, as $[\mathrm{Al(H_2O)_6}]^{3+}$. The $\mathrm{3+}$ charge on a small ion polarises (pulls on) the electrons of the O-H bonds in the water ligands, so a proton is lost fairly easily:"),
        F(r"[\mathrm{Al(H_2O)_6}]^{3+} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons [\mathrm{Al(H_2O)_5(OH)}]^{2+} + \mathrm{H_3O^+}"),
        T(r"So solutions of $\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$, $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ salts are acidic. Larger ions of lower charge, such as $\mathrm{Na^+}$ and $\mathrm{K^+}$, do not polarise water enough to hydrolyse it. If you add a carbonate to aluminium chloride solution you see a white precipitate and bubbles, because the acidic ion reacts with the basic carbonate ion: $2\mathrm{Al^{3+}} + 3\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}} + 3\mathrm{H_2O} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Al(OH)_3} + 3\mathrm{CO_2}$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: finding Ka from the pH of a salt solution", intro=r"A $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ solution of the sodium salt NaA of a weak acid HA has pH $8.90$. Calculate $K_a$ of HA. ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$.)", steps=[
            S("pOH and hydroxide ions", math=r"\text{pOH} = 14.00 - 8.90 = 5.10,\qquad [\mathrm{OH^-}] = 10^{-5.10} = 7.94\times10^{-6}"),
            S("Hydrolysis of $\\mathrm{A^-}$ gives $K_b$", math=r"K_b = \frac{[\mathrm{OH^-}]^2}{c} = \frac{(7.94\times10^{-6})^2}{0.100} = 6.31\times10^{-10}"),
            S("Convert to Ka", math=r"K_a = \frac{K_w}{K_b} = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{6.31\times10^{-10}} = 1.6\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using Ka of the acid instead of Kb of the anion", r"For $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$ the constant that goes into $\sqrt{K\,c}$ is $K_b = K_w/K_a$, because the ion acts as a base."),
        QUIZ(r"Which solution (all $0.1\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$) has the lowest pH?",
             [r"$\mathrm{NaCl}$", r"$\mathrm{CH_3COONa}$", r"$\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$", r"$\mathrm{Na_2CO_3}$"], 2,
             r"$\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ donates protons to water, so $\mathrm{NH_4Cl}$ is acidic. $\mathrm{NaCl}$ is neutral and the other two are alkaline.",
             feedback=["NaCl is neutral (pH about 7).", "Ethanoate is a base, so the solution is alkaline.", "", "Carbonate is a base, so the solution is alkaline."], hint="Which ion is the conjugate acid of a weak base?"),
        QUIZ(r"Why is a solution of aluminium chloride acidic?",
             [r"Chloride ions react with water to form HCl", r"The hydrated $\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$ ion polarises water ligands and releases $\mathrm{H^+}$", r"Aluminium ions react with $\mathrm{OH^-}$ in water to form a precipitate and nothing else", r"Aluminium chloride contains hydrochloric acid"], 1,
             r"$[\mathrm{Al(H_2O)_6}]^{3+}$ acts as a weak acid.",
             feedback=["Chloride is the conjugate base of a strong acid; it does not react with water.", "", "This does not explain the lower pH; it is the loss of H+ from the hydrated ion that matters.", "AlCl3 is a salt; it does not contain free HCl."], hint="Think about the charge density of the small $\\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$ ion."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Predict whether each solution is acidic, alkaline or neutral, and give the reaction responsible where there is one: (a) $\mathrm{KNO_3}$, (b) $\mathrm{NH_4NO_3}$, (c) $\mathrm{Na_2CO_3}$, (d) $\mathrm{FeCl_3}$.",
           r"(a) Neutral: both ions come from a strong acid and a strong base. (b) Acidic: $\mathrm{NH_4^+} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{NH_3} + \mathrm{H_3O^+}$. (c) Alkaline: $\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{HCO_3^-} + \mathrm{OH^-}$. (d) Acidic: $[\mathrm{Fe(H_2O)_6}]^{3+}$ loses a proton in the same way as the aluminium ion."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the pH of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ sodium ethanoate ($K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}$; $K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$).",
           r"$K_b = 5.56\times10^{-10}$. $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{5.56\times10^{-10}\times0.200} = 1.05\times10^{-5}$. $\text{pOH} = 4.98$. $\text{pH} = 14.00 - 4.98 = 9.02$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why ammonium ethanoate solution is almost neutral, although it is the salt of a weak acid and a weak base.",
           r"The ammonium ion acts as an acid, and the ethanoate ion acts as a base. Because $K_a(\mathrm{NH_4^+}) = K_w/K_b(\mathrm{NH_3})$ and $K_b(\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}) = K_w/K_a(\mathrm{CH_3COOH})$, and $K_a$ of ethanoic acid is the same as $K_b$ of ammonia ($1.8\times10^{-5}$), the two ions produce $\mathrm{H^+}$ and $\mathrm{OH^-}$ to the same extent, so the pH is close to 7."),
        PQ(r"A $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ solution of sodium salt NaA has pH $8.90$. Calculate $K_a$ of the acid HA.",
           r"$\text{pOH} = 5.10$, $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 7.94\times10^{-6}$. $K_b = \frac{(7.94\times10^{-6})^2}{0.100} = 6.3\times10^{-10}$. $K_a = \frac{1.00\times10^{-14}}{6.3\times10^{-10}} = 1.6\times10^{-5}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: salt hydrolysis appears in Paper 2 (explain the pH of salt solutions with equations, with a calculation) and in Paper 1 (rank or identify acidic and alkaline salts). It links to the equivalence-point pH in titrations."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Salts of a strong acid and a strong base are neutral. A weak acid with a strong base gives an alkaline solution. A strong acid with a weak base gives an acidic solution.",
            r"The anion of a weak acid is a weak base: $\mathrm{A^-} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{HA} + \mathrm{OH^-}$, with $K_b = K_w / K_a$.",
            r"The cation of a weak base is a weak acid: $\mathrm{BH^+} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{B} + \mathrm{H_3O^+}$, with $K_a = K_w / K_b$.",
            r"Small, highly charged cations ($\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$, $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$) make solutions acidic because the hydrated ion loses a proton.",
            r"Then use $[\mathrm{H^+}] = \sqrt{K_a c}$ or $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \sqrt{K_b c}$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 13. Solubility product and the common ion effect
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ION, topic="Solubility product and the common ion effect",
    title="Solubility product, solubility and the common ion effect",
    slug="chu1-ksp-common-ion",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Write Ksp expressions, calculate solubility from Ksp and Ksp from solubility, predict precipitation using the ionic product, and explain how a common ion lowers solubility.",
    objectives=[
        "Write the solubility product expression and units for sparingly soluble salts",
        "Calculate Ksp from solubility and solubility from Ksp, including in g per dm3",
        "Predict whether a precipitate forms by comparing the ionic product with Ksp",
        "Calculate the effect of a common ion on solubility, and explain the effect of pH on the solubility of a hydroxide",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Solubility product Ksp", "Solubility", "Ionic product", "Precipitation", "Common ion effect", "Solubility and pH"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Hard water leaves a white scale in kettles and pipes. That scale is a solid that dissolved only slightly in the water and then came out of solution. Treatment plants add chemicals to remove metal ions from wastewater by making them precipitate. Both are examples of **sparingly soluble salts**, which dissolve a little, and then reach an equilibrium between the solid and its ions."),
        T(r"When solid silver chloride is shaken with water, a tiny amount dissolves and a dynamic equilibrium is reached between solid and ions in a **saturated solution**:"),
        F(r"\mathrm{AgCl(s)} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Ag^+(aq)} + \mathrm{Cl^-(aq)}"),
        D(r"The **solubility product** $K_{sp}$ is the product of the concentrations of the ions in a saturated solution, each raised to the power of its coefficient in the equation. The solid is left out of the expression.", title="Solubility product",
          formula=r"K_{sp}(\mathrm{AgCl}) = [\mathrm{Ag^+}][\mathrm{Cl^-}]",
          note="Ksp changes only with temperature. Its units depend on the formula."),
        CODE("""Salt        Equilibrium                      Ksp expression          Units of Ksp
AgCl        AgCl <=> Ag+ + Cl-               [Ag+][Cl-]              mol2 dm-6
PbI2        PbI2 <=> Pb2+ + 2I-              [Pb2+][I-]^2            mol3 dm-9
Mg(OH)2     Mg(OH)2 <=> Mg2+ + 2OH-          [Mg2+][OH-]^2           mol3 dm-9
Ag2CrO4     Ag2CrO4 <=> 2Ag+ + CrO4 2-       [Ag+]^2[CrO4 2-]        mol3 dm-9""", "text", title="Ksp expressions and units"),
        T(r"We use these values (at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$; sources differ slightly, so check your textbook): $K_{sp}(\mathrm{AgCl}) = 1.8\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{mol^2\,dm^{-6}}$, $K_{sp}(\mathrm{PbI_2}) = 7.1\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{mol^3\,dm^{-9}}$, $K_{sp}(\mathrm{Mg(OH)_2}) = 1.8\times10^{-11}\ \mathrm{mol^3\,dm^{-9}}$."),
        D(r"The **solubility** $s$ is the concentration of the dissolved salt in a saturated solution (mol per dm3). For a salt giving $x$ cations and $y$ anions, each ion concentration is $x s$ or $y s$.", title="Solubility"),
        EX("Worked example 1: solubility of silver chloride and of lead(II) iodide", intro=r"Calculate the solubility of (a) AgCl, in $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ and $\mathrm{g\,dm^{-3}}$ ($M_r = 143.5$), and (b) $\mathrm{PbI_2}$, in $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, from the $K_{sp}$ values above.", steps=[
            S("(a) AgCl: $[\\mathrm{Ag^+}] = [\\mathrm{Cl^-}] = s$", math=r"K_{sp} = s^2 \Rightarrow s = \sqrt{1.8\times10^{-10}} = 1.34\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Convert to g per dm3", math=r"1.34\times10^{-5}\times143.5 = 1.9\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{g\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("(b) $\\mathrm{PbI_2}$: $[\\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}] = s$ and $[\\mathrm{I^-}] = 2s$", math=r"K_{sp} = s(2s)^2 = 4s^3"),
            S("Solve for s", math=r"s = \sqrt[3]{\frac{7.1\times10^{-9}}{4}} = \sqrt[3]{1.775\times10^{-9}} = 1.21\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
        ], check=r"4(1.21\times10^{-3})^3 = 7.1\times10^{-9}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: Ksp from a measured solubility", intro=r"A saturated solution of lead(II) iodide contains $0.562\ \mathrm{g}$ of solid dissolved per dm3. ($M_r = 461$.) Calculate $K_{sp}$.", steps=[
            S("Solubility in mol per dm3", math=r"s = \frac{0.562}{461} = 1.22\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Ion concentrations", math=r"[\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}] = 1.22\times10^{-3},\qquad [\mathrm{I^-}] = 2.44\times10^{-3}"),
            S("Ksp", math=r"K_{sp} = (1.22\times10^{-3})(2.44\times10^{-3})^2 = 4(1.22\times10^{-3})^3 = 7.3\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{mol^3\,dm^{-9}}"),
        ]),
        H("The common ion effect"),
        D(r"The **common ion effect** is the decrease in solubility of a sparingly soluble salt when a solution already contains one of its ions (from another, soluble, source).", title="Common ion effect"),
        T(r"Adding $\mathrm{Cl^-}$ from NaCl to a saturated AgCl solution raises $[\mathrm{Cl^-}]$. By Le Chatelier's principle the equilibrium $\mathrm{AgCl(s)} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Ag^+} + \mathrm{Cl^-}$ shifts to the left, so some AgCl precipitates and the solubility falls. $K_{sp}$ itself stays the same."),
        EX("Worked example 3: AgCl in sodium chloride solution", intro=r"Calculate the solubility of AgCl in $0.0100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaCl, and compare with its solubility in pure water.", steps=[
            S("Chloride ions come almost entirely from NaCl", math=r"[\mathrm{Cl^-}] \approx 0.0100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Use Ksp to find silver ions. The dissolved silver ion concentration equals the solubility", math=r"s = [\mathrm{Ag^+}] = \frac{K_{sp}}{[\mathrm{Cl^-}]} = \frac{1.8\times10^{-10}}{0.0100} = 1.8\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Compare", text="In pure water s = 1.34e-5 mol per dm3, so the solubility is about 750 times smaller. The approximation is valid: the chloride from AgCl (1.8e-8) is negligible next to 0.0100."),
        ]),
        H("Will a precipitate form? The ionic product"),
        T(r"The **ionic product** $Q$ has the same form as $K_{sp}$ but uses the concentrations at that moment, not necessarily at equilibrium. If $Q > K_{sp}$ the solution is supersaturated, and a precipitate forms until $Q = K_{sp}$. If $Q < K_{sp}$ no precipitate forms. When you mix two solutions, **remember to calculate the concentrations after mixing**."),
        EX("Worked example 4: mixing silver nitrate and sodium chloride", intro=r"$50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $1.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{AgNO_3}$ is mixed with $50.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $1.0\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaCl. Will AgCl precipitate? ($K_{sp} = 1.8\times10^{-10}$.)", steps=[
            S("After mixing, each solution is diluted to twice its volume", math=r"[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = [\mathrm{Cl^-}] = \frac{1.0\times10^{-3}}{2} = 5.0\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Ionic product", math=r"Q = (5.0\times10^{-4})(5.0\times10^{-4}) = 2.5\times10^{-7}"),
            S("Compare with Ksp", text="Q = 2.5e-7 is much larger than 1.8e-10, so AgCl precipitates."),
        ]),
        H("The effect of pH on the solubility of a hydroxide"),
        T(r"$\mathrm{Mg(OH)_2}$ is a hydroxide, so its solubility depends on $[\mathrm{OH^-}]$. In pure water, $K_{sp} = s(2s)^2 = 4s^3$ gives $s = \sqrt[3]{1.8\times10^{-11}/4} = 1.65\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 3.30\times10^{-4}$ and $\text{pH} = 10.52$. In a solution buffered at a **lower** pH (for example in acid) the $\mathrm{OH^-}$ ions are removed, the equilibrium shifts right, and the solubility rises. In a solution with a higher pH (more $\mathrm{OH^-}$, a common ion) the solubility falls."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the coefficient in $4s^3$", r"For $\mathrm{PbI_2}$ the iodide concentration is $2s$, and it is squared: $K_{sp} = s(2s)^2 = 4s^3$. Writing $s^3$ gives an answer that is wrong by a factor of about 1.6."),
        QUIZ(r"What is the $K_{sp}$ expression for $\mathrm{Ag_2CrO_4}$?",
             [r"$[\mathrm{Ag^+}][\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]$", r"$[\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2[\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]$", r"$2[\mathrm{Ag^+}][\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]$", r"$[\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2[\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]^2$"], 1,
             r"$\mathrm{Ag_2CrO_4(s)} \rightleftharpoons 2\mathrm{Ag^+} + \mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}$, so $K_{sp} = [\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2[\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]$.",
             feedback=["The coefficient 2 gives a power of 2.", "", "A coefficient becomes a power, not a multiplier.", "Only the silver ion is squared."], hint="Write the dissolution equation first."),
        QUIZ(r"Sodium chloride is added to a saturated solution of AgCl. What happens?",
             [r"The solubility of AgCl rises because there are more ions", r"$K_{sp}$ falls", r"More AgCl precipitates, because $[\mathrm{Cl^-}]$ has risen", r"Nothing: NaCl and AgCl are different substances"], 2,
             r"The common ion $\mathrm{Cl^-}$ shifts the equilibrium left, so AgCl precipitates and its solubility is lower.",
             feedback=["A common ion lowers solubility.", "Ksp depends only on temperature.", "", "NaCl supplies Cl-, which is a common ion."], hint="Which ion do the two salts share?"),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The solubility of silver chromate, $\mathrm{Ag_2CrO_4}$, in water at $25\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ is $4.3\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. Write the $K_{sp}$ expression and calculate $K_{sp}$.",
           r"$K_{sp} = [\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2[\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}]$. $[\mathrm{CrO_4^{2-}}] = 4.3\times10^{-5}$ and $[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = 8.6\times10^{-5}$. $K_{sp} = (8.6\times10^{-5})^2(4.3\times10^{-5}) = 4s^3 = 3.2\times10^{-13}\ \mathrm{mol^3\,dm^{-9}}$."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the solubility of $\mathrm{Mg(OH)_2}$ in water ($K_{sp} = 1.8\times10^{-11}$) and the pH of the saturated solution ($K_w = 1.00\times10^{-14}$).",
           r"$K_{sp} = 4s^3$, so $s = \sqrt[3]{4.5\times10^{-12}} = 1.65\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. $[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 3.30\times10^{-4}$, $\text{pOH} = 3.48$, $\text{pH} = 10.52$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why lead(II) iodide is less soluble in $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ potassium iodide than in pure water, and calculate its solubility in this solution ($K_{sp} = 7.1\times10^{-9}$).",
           r"Potassium iodide supplies iodide ions, a common ion. The equilibrium $\mathrm{PbI_2(s)} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Pb^{2+}} + 2\mathrm{I^-}$ shifts to the left. Take $[\mathrm{I^-}] \approx 0.10$. Then $s = [\mathrm{Pb^{2+}}] = \frac{K_{sp}}{[\mathrm{I^-}]^2} = \frac{7.1\times10^{-9}}{0.010} = 7.1\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, much lower than $1.2\times10^{-3}$."),
        PQ(r"A solution contains $0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ silver ions. What is the minimum chloride ion concentration needed to start the precipitation of AgCl? ($K_{sp} = 1.8\times10^{-10}$)",
           r"AgCl starts to precipitate when $Q = K_{sp}$: $[\mathrm{Cl^-}] = \frac{1.8\times10^{-10}}{0.010} = 1.8\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: Ksp calculations (solubility, common ion, precipitation) are standard Paper 2 items. Paper 1 asks which expression or which effect. State the units of Ksp when you calculate it, and use the total volume after mixing."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$K_{sp}$ is the product of the ion concentrations (each to the power of its coefficient) in a saturated solution. The solid is not included.",
            r"Solubility from Ksp: $s = \sqrt{K_{sp}}$ for a 1:1 salt and $s = \sqrt[3]{K_{sp}/4}$ for an $\mathrm{AB_2}$ salt. Convert to g per dm3 with the molar mass.",
            r"A common ion lowers solubility: for AgCl in chloride, $s = K_{sp}/[\mathrm{Cl^-}]$. $K_{sp}$ is unchanged.",
            r"Precipitation occurs when the ionic product $Q$ exceeds $K_{sp}$. Use concentrations after mixing.",
            "Hydroxides are more soluble in acid, because H+ removes OH-."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 14. Standard electrode potentials
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ELE, topic="Standard electrode potentials",
    title="The standard hydrogen electrode and standard electrode potentials",
    slug="chu1-standard-electrode-potentials",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Describe the standard hydrogen electrode and standard conditions, define E-standard, draw and label an electrochemical cell with a salt bridge, write cell diagrams, and use a table of E-standard values to rank oxidising and reducing agents.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the standard hydrogen electrode and state standard conditions",
        "Define standard electrode potential and explain why a reference electrode is needed",
        "Describe the three types of half-cell and write cell diagrams with correct conventions",
        "Use E-standard values to compare oxidising and reducing agents",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Half-cell", "Standard hydrogen electrode", "Standard electrode potential", "Salt bridge", "Cell diagram", "Electrochemical series"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A phone battery, a car battery and a torch cell all make electricity from a chemical reaction. In each, one substance gives up electrons and another takes them. Which substance is more ready to take electrons? We answer that with a number, the **electrode potential**, measured in volts."),
        T(r"A redox reaction can be split into two **half-equations**: oxidation (loss of electrons) and reduction (gain of electrons). If the two halves are placed in separate containers, called **half-cells**, and joined by a wire and a **salt bridge**, electrons flow through the wire from the half-cell where oxidation occurs to the one where reduction occurs. This is an **electrochemical cell**."),
        IMG("al-chu1-daniell-cell.svg", alt="A zinc rod in zinc sulfate solution on the left and a copper rod in copper sulfate solution on the right, joined by a salt bridge and by a wire containing a voltmeter. An arrow shows electrons flowing along the wire from the zinc to the copper.", caption="Figure 1. The zinc-copper cell (Daniell cell). Electrons leave the zinc (anode) and arrive at the copper (cathode)."),
        KEY("Anode and cathode", "These definitions never change.", cards=[
            ["Anode", r"Where **oxidation** happens. In a cell (a source of electricity) it is the negative terminal: $\mathrm{Zn} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + 2e^-$."],
            ["Cathode", r"Where **reduction** happens. In a cell it is the positive terminal: $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{Cu}$."],
            ["Salt bridge", r"A strip of paper soaked in a salt solution such as potassium nitrate (or a tube of it). It lets ions move to complete the circuit and keep each half-cell neutral, without letting the solutions mix. The salt must not react with either solution (so no KCl with silver ions)."],
        ]),
        T(r"The voltage of a cell is the *difference* between its two half-cells. We cannot measure the potential of a single half-cell alone, because we always need another half-cell to complete the circuit. So we choose one half-cell as the reference, give it the value zero, and measure all others against it."),
        D(r"The **standard hydrogen electrode (SHE)** is the reference half-cell. It consists of a platinum electrode coated with finely divided platinum (platinum black), in a solution of $\mathrm{H^+}$ of concentration $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ at $298\ \mathrm{K}$, with hydrogen gas bubbling over it at a pressure of $1\ \mathrm{bar}$ ($100\ \mathrm{kPa}$). By definition, $E^\circ = 0.00\ \mathrm{V}$.", title="Standard hydrogen electrode",
          formula=r"2\mathrm{H^+(aq)} + 2e^- \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H_2(g)} \qquad E^\circ = 0.00\ \mathrm{V}",
          note="The platinum is inert: it conducts electrons and adsorbs hydrogen gas. Check your textbook: older books use 1 atm instead of 1 bar."),
        D(r"The **standard electrode potential** $E^\circ$ of a half-cell is the e.m.f. of a cell in which the half-cell, under standard conditions, is the right-hand electrode and the standard hydrogen electrode is the left-hand electrode. Standard conditions: $298\ \mathrm{K}$, solutions at $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, gases at $1\ \mathrm{bar}$. The half-equation is written as a **reduction**.", title="Standard electrode potential"),
        KEY("What the half-cell contains", "Every half-cell has the oxidised and the reduced form.", cards=[
            [r"Metal / metal ion", r"A rod of the metal in a $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ solution of its ions: $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}$, $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$."],
            [r"Ion / ion", r"Two ions of the same element in one solution, each at $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, with a platinum electrode: $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}}$."],
            [r"Gas / ion", r"A platinum electrode with the gas bubbling over it, in a solution of its ions: $\mathrm{Cl_2/Cl^-}$ and $\mathrm{H^+/H_2}$."],
        ]),
        H("Cell diagrams"),
        T(r"A **cell diagram** is a short way to describe a cell. A vertical line | means a phase boundary and a double line || means the salt bridge. By convention the **left** half-cell is where oxidation takes place and the **right** half-cell is where reduction takes place. The cell e.m.f. is $E^\circ_{\text{right}} - E^\circ_{\text{left}}$."),
        CODE("""Zn(s) | Zn2+(aq) || Cu2+(aq) | Cu(s)                    E = +1.10 V
Pt(s) | H2(g) | H+(aq) || Fe3+(aq), Fe2+(aq) | Pt(s)       E = +0.77 V
Cu(s) | Cu2+(aq) || Ag+(aq) | Ag(s)                       E = +0.46 V""", "text", title="Cell diagrams (standard conditions; values from the table below)"),
        H("The electrochemical series"),
        CODE("""Half-equation (reduction)                    E-standard / V
Mg2+ + 2e-  <=>  Mg                          -2.37
Al3+ + 3e-  <=>  Al                          -1.66
Zn2+ + 2e-  <=>  Zn                          -0.76
Fe2+ + 2e-  <=>  Fe                          -0.44
Pb2+ + 2e-  <=>  Pb                          -0.13
2H+  + 2e-  <=>  H2                           0.00
Cu2+ + 2e-  <=>  Cu                          +0.34
I2   + 2e-  <=>  2I-                         +0.54
Fe3+ +  e-  <=>  Fe2+                        +0.77
Ag+  +  e-  <=>  Ag                          +0.80
Br2  + 2e-  <=>  2Br-                        +1.07
Cr2O7 2- + 14H+ + 6e- <=> 2Cr3+ + 7H2O       +1.33
Cl2  + 2e-  <=>  2Cl-                        +1.36
MnO4- + 8H+ + 5e- <=> Mn2+ + 4H2O            +1.51""", "text", title="Standard electrode potentials at 298 K"),
        T(r"Read the table like this. The more **negative** $E^\circ$, the more the left-hand species (for example $\mathrm{Mg}$) tends to give up electrons, so it is a **strong reducing agent**. The more **positive** $E^\circ$, the more the left-hand species (for example $\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$) tends to gain electrons, so it is a **strong oxidising agent**. The strongest reducing agent is at the top right, and the strongest oxidising agent is at the bottom left."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a zinc half-cell against the SHE", intro=r"A zinc half-cell is connected to the SHE. The voltmeter reads $0.76\ \mathrm{V}$, and the zinc electrode is the negative terminal. State the $E^\circ$ of $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}$ and write the cell reaction.", steps=[
            S("Zinc gives electrons to the circuit, so it is oxidised and is the anode (left)", text="The cell diagram is Zn(s) | Zn2+(aq) || H+(aq) | H2(g) | Pt(s)."),
            S("Cell e.m.f.", math=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ_{\text{right}} - E^\circ_{\text{left}} = 0.00 - E^\circ(\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}) = +0.76\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Solve", math=r"E^\circ(\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}) = -0.76\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Cell reaction", math=r"\mathrm{Zn} + 2\mathrm{H^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + \mathrm{H_2}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: designing the Fe3+/Fe2+ half-cell", intro=r"Describe the half-cell needed to measure the standard electrode potential of $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}}$.", steps=[
            S("There is no metal to use as an electrode, so use platinum", text="Platinum is inert. It carries electrons to and from the ions."),
            S("Solution", text=r"A solution containing both $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ ions, each at $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ (for example iron(III) sulfate and iron(II) sulfate)."),
            S("Complete the circuit", text="A salt bridge joins it to the SHE, and a high-resistance voltmeter measures the e.m.f. (+0.77 V). The temperature is 298 K."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: ranking with the series", intro=r"Using the table, (a) place $\mathrm{Ag^+}$, $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$, $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ in order of increasing strength as oxidising agents. (b) Which of Zn, Cu, Ag is the strongest reducing agent?", steps=[
            S("(a) Order by $E^\\circ$ from lowest to highest", math=r"\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}\,(-0.76) < \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}\,(+0.34) < \mathrm{Fe^{3+}}\,(+0.77) < \mathrm{Ag^+}\,(+0.80)"),
            S("(b) The most negative potential means the metal gives up electrons most easily", text="Zinc is the strongest reducing agent of the three."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Multiplying E-standard by the number of electrons", r"$E^\circ$ is a potential per charge, not per mole of reaction. Doubling a half-equation does **not** double its $E^\circ$. $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^- \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Cu}$ is $+0.34$ V whether you write it once or twice."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that E-standard is for the reduction", r"The table gives potentials for reduction half-equations. If you reverse the half-equation to write an oxidation, the sign of the $E^\circ$ is reversed: $\mathrm{Zn} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + 2e^-$ has $+0.76$ V as an oxidation potential."),
        QUIZ(r"Which statement about the standard hydrogen electrode is correct?",
             [r"It has $E^\circ = +1.00\ \mathrm{V}$ and uses zinc", r"It uses platinum, hydrogen at 1 bar and $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}\ \mathrm{H^+}$, and has $E^\circ = 0.00\ \mathrm{V}$ by definition", r"It uses a hydrogen electrode in $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl at 373 K", r"Its potential depends on the second half-cell"], 1,
             r"The SHE is the reference. Platinum is inert, hydrogen is at 1 bar, and the acid is at $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ at 298 K.",
             feedback=["Zinc is not used, and its value is defined as zero.", "", "The temperature of the standard electrode is 298 K.", "The SHE is defined as 0.00 V, and it does not depend on anything else."], hint="Recall the conditions: concentration, pressure and temperature."),
        QUIZ(r"The $E^\circ$ values for $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}/Fe}$ and $\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}$ are $-0.44\ \mathrm{V}$ and $+0.80\ \mathrm{V}$. Which is the stronger reducing agent: Fe or Ag?",
             ["Ag, because its E-standard is larger", "Fe, because its E-standard is more negative", "They are equally strong", "Neither: metals cannot be reducing agents"], 1,
             r"A more negative $E^\circ$ means the metal loses electrons more easily, so iron is the stronger reducing agent.",
             feedback=["The more positive the $E^\\circ$, the stronger the oxidising agent (Ag+), not the reducing agent.", "", "They differ by 1.24 V.", "Metals are the commonest reducing agents."], hint="Which metal gives up electrons more readily?"),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Describe how you would measure the standard electrode potential of $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$, and state what the voltmeter reading would be.",
           r"Set up a copper electrode in $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ and connect it with a salt bridge (for example filter paper soaked in $\mathrm{KNO_3}$) to a standard hydrogen electrode (platinum black in $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{H^+}$ with hydrogen at 1 bar) at $298\ \mathrm{K}$. Connect the electrodes through a high-resistance voltmeter. The reading is $+0.34\ \mathrm{V}$, with copper as the positive terminal."),
        PQ(r"Write the cell diagram for the cell made from $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$ and $\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}$ half-cells, and calculate $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$.",
           r"Copper has the lower $E^\circ$ (+0.34 V), so it is oxidised and is on the left: $\mathrm{Cu(s)} | \mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq)} \,||\, \mathrm{Ag^+(aq)} | \mathrm{Ag(s)}$. $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = +0.80 - (+0.34) = +0.46\ \mathrm{V}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain the purpose of the salt bridge, and say why a solution of potassium chloride would not be suitable in the silver half-cell.",
           r"The salt bridge completes the electrical circuit by letting ions move between the half-cells, which also keeps each half-cell electrically neutral, without allowing the two solutions to mix. Chloride ions from KCl would react with $\mathrm{Ag^+}$ to give a precipitate of AgCl, removing the silver ions and altering the electrode potential."),
        PQ(r"Use the table to arrange $\mathrm{Br_2}$, $\mathrm{I_2}$, $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ in order of oxidising power, and say which halide ion is the best reducing agent.",
           r"$E^\circ$: $\mathrm{I_2/I^-}$ $0.54$, $\mathrm{Br_2/Br^-}$ $1.07$, $\mathrm{Cl_2/Cl^-}$ $1.36$. Oxidising power increases in the order $\mathrm{I_2} < \mathrm{Br_2} < \mathrm{Cl_2}$. The best reducing agent is the ion with the most negative (lowest) $E^\circ$ for its half-equation, which is $\mathrm{I^-}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: standard electrode potentials, the SHE, labelled cell diagrams and the use of E-standard to compare oxidising and reducing agents are Paper 2 items. In Paper 1 they appear as multiple-choice items using a data table. In practical work the apparatus is a voltmeter, metal strips and filter-paper salt bridges."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"The SHE ($\mathrm{H^+}$ $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$, $\mathrm{H_2}$ 1 bar, platinum, 298 K) is the reference with $E^\circ = 0.00\ \mathrm{V}$.",
            r"$E^\circ$ of a half-cell is the e.m.f. of the cell with the SHE on the left; half-equations are written as reductions; standard conditions are 298 K, 1.00 mol per dm3 and 1 bar.",
            "Oxidation at the anode (negative terminal of a cell); reduction at the cathode (positive). The salt bridge completes the circuit.",
            "Three types of half-cell: metal/ion, ion/ion with platinum, gas/ion with platinum. Cell diagram: oxidation on the left, reduction on the right.",
            r"More negative $E^\circ$ = stronger reducing agent; more positive $E^\circ$ = stronger oxidising agent. Do not multiply $E^\circ$ by coefficients."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 15. Cell EMF and spontaneity
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ELE, topic="Cell EMF and spontaneity",
    title="Cell e.m.f., feasibility, free energy and equilibrium constants",
    slug="chu1-cell-emf-spontaneity",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Calculate the standard e.m.f. of a cell, write the cell reaction, predict feasibility, link E-standard to delta G and to K, and recognise when a feasible reaction may still not occur.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate E-standard of a cell from two half-cell potentials and write the overall equation",
        "Predict whether a redox reaction is feasible under standard conditions",
        "Use delta G = -nFE and ln K = nFE/RT",
        "Explain why a positive E-standard does not guarantee a reaction",
    ],
    key_concepts=["E-standard of a cell", "Cell reaction", "Feasibility", "delta G and E", "Equilibrium constant from E", "Kinetic limits"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Why can a zinc spoon dissolve in copper sulfate solution but not the other way round? Why does iron rust in the open air while gold does not? The table of standard electrode potentials lets us **predict** which redox reactions will go, before we do any experiment."),
        H("Calculating the e.m.f. of a cell"),
        D(r"The standard e.m.f. of a cell is the standard electrode potential of the half-cell where **reduction** occurs (the cathode, more positive) minus that of the half-cell where **oxidation** occurs (the anode, more negative):", title="Standard cell e.m.f.",
          formula=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ_{\text{reduction}} - E^\circ_{\text{oxidation}} = E^\circ_{\text{right}} - E^\circ_{\text{left}}",
          note="Both values are taken from the table as reduction potentials. Never multiply an E-standard by the number of electrons."),
        KEY("Method for the cell reaction", "Follow the same four steps every time.", cards=[
            ["1. Choose", "The half-cell with the more positive E-standard is reduced. The other is oxidised."],
            ["2. Write", "Write the reduction half-equation as in the table. Reverse the other half-equation."],
            ["3. Balance electrons", "Multiply the half-equations by whole numbers so that the electrons cancel. Do not change the E-standard values."],
            ["4. Add and subtract", "Add the half-equations for the cell reaction. Calculate E-standard of the cell = (more positive) minus (less positive)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: the zinc-copper cell", intro=r"Find the cell reaction and $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ for the cell made from $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}/Zn}$ ($-0.76\ \mathrm{V}$) and $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$ ($+0.34\ \mathrm{V}$). Then find $\Delta G^\circ$ and $K$. (Use $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$, $T = 298\ \mathrm{K}$.)", steps=[
            S("Copper is more positive, so $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ is reduced and Zn is oxidised", math=r"\mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{Cu}, \qquad \mathrm{Zn} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + 2e^-"),
            S("Add", math=r"\mathrm{Zn(s)} + \mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq)} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}(aq)} + \mathrm{Cu(s)}"),
            S("E-standard of the cell", math=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = +0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("$n = 2$ electrons, so free energy change", math=r"\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ = -2\times96\,500\times1.10 = -2.12\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = -212\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
            S("Equilibrium constant", math=r"\ln K = \frac{nFE^\circ}{RT} = \frac{2\times96\,500\times1.10}{8.31\times298} = 85.7 \Rightarrow K = 1.7\times10^{37}"),
        ], check=r"\Delta G^\circ = -RT\ln K = -8.31\times298\times85.73 = -2.12\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}"),
        H("Free energy and equilibrium"),
        D(r"A reaction is feasible (thermodynamically spontaneous) when $\Delta G < 0$. For a redox reaction with $n$ moles of electrons transferred per mole of reaction:", title="E, delta G and K",
          formula=r"\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ_{\text{cell}}, \qquad \ln K = \frac{nFE^\circ_{\text{cell}}}{RT}",
          note="F = 96 500 C per mole. A positive E-standard gives a negative delta G and a K greater than 1. At 298 K, log10 K = nE/0.0591."),
        T(r"So a large positive $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ means the reaction goes almost to completion. A negative $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ means $K < 1$ and the reactants are favoured. A value close to zero means a mixture of substantial amounts of both."),
        EX("Worked example 2: silver ions and zinc, and the role of n", intro=r"Find the cell reaction, $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ and $\Delta G^\circ$ for $\mathrm{Zn}$ and $\mathrm{Ag^+}$ ($E^\circ(\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}) = +0.80\ \mathrm{V}$).", steps=[
            S("Silver ions are reduced. Multiply the silver half-equation by 2 so that the electrons balance", math=r"2\mathrm{Ag^+} + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Ag}, \qquad \mathrm{Zn} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + 2e^-"),
            S("Add", math=r"\mathrm{Zn(s)} + 2\mathrm{Ag^+(aq)} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}(aq)} + 2\mathrm{Ag(s)}"),
            S("E-standard is **not** doubled", math=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = +0.80 - (-0.76) = +1.56\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Free energy, $n = 2$", math=r"\Delta G^\circ = -2\times96\,500\times1.56 = -3.01\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = -301\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        H("Predicting whether a reaction will go"),
        EX("Worked example 3: three predictions", intro=r"Use the table of $E^\circ$ values to decide whether each reaction is feasible under standard conditions. (a) $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ with $\mathrm{I^-}$. (b) Copper with $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ hydrochloric acid. (c) Acidified $\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ with $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$.", steps=[
            S("(a) $\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}} = +0.77$ is higher than $\\mathrm{I_2/I^-} = +0.54$", math=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 0.77 - 0.54 = +0.23\ \mathrm{V}: \quad 2\mathrm{Fe^{3+}} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + \mathrm{I_2}"),
            S("(b) For Cu to react, $\\mathrm{H^+}$ would have to be reduced and Cu oxidised", math=r"E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ(\mathrm{H^+/H_2}) - E^\circ(\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu}) = 0.00 - 0.34 = -0.34\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Conclusion for (b)", text="Negative, so copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid."),
            S("(c) $\\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ is reduced, $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ oxidised", math=r"\mathrm{MnO_4^-} + 8\mathrm{H^+} + 5\mathrm{Fe^{2+}} \rightarrow \mathrm{Mn^{2+}} + 4\mathrm{H_2O} + 5\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}, \quad E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.51 - 0.77 = +0.74\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Free energy, $n = 5$", math=r"\Delta G^\circ = -5\times96\,500\times0.74 = -357\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The equation in (c) is the basis of the manganate(VII) titration for iron. Check: left charge $= -1 + 8 + 10 = +17$; right charge $= +2 + 15 = +17$. Atoms: Mn 1, O 4 and 4, H 8 and 8, Fe 5 and 5."),
        H("Limits of the predictions"),
        KEY("A positive E-standard does not guarantee a reaction", "Three reasons:", cards=[
            ["Not standard conditions", "E depends on concentration and temperature. A small positive E-standard can become negative at very different concentrations (the next lesson)."],
            ["Kinetics", r"E tells us the reaction is **possible**, not that it is **fast**. $\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ could oxidise water (1.51 V against 1.23 V for $\mathrm{O_2/H_2O}$), yet a permanganate solution lasts for days because the activation energy is very high. Aluminium resists acid because of a thin oxide layer."],
            ["Equation written", r"E-standard does not depend on how the equation is written or scaled, but $\Delta G$ does (through $n$)."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Doubling E-standard when doubling the equation", r"E-standard is not multiplied. Only the number of electrons $n$ in $\Delta G = -nFE$ changes."),
        MISTAKE("Subtracting the wrong way round", r"$E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ is (more positive) minus (less positive), so a feasible reaction gives a positive result. If you get a negative number for a working cell, you subtracted the wrong way."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a feasible reaction is fast", r"A positive $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ means the reaction is thermodynamically favourable. Rate depends on activation energy, so some feasible reactions need a catalyst or heating."),
        QUIZ(r"$E^\circ(\mathrm{Fe^{2+}/Fe}) = -0.44\ \mathrm{V}$ and $E^\circ(\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}) = +0.80\ \mathrm{V}$. What is $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ for $\mathrm{Fe} + 2\mathrm{Ag^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + 2\mathrm{Ag}$?",
             [r"$+0.36\ \mathrm{V}$", r"$+1.24\ \mathrm{V}$", r"$+2.04\ \mathrm{V}$", r"$-1.24\ \mathrm{V}$"], 1,
             r"$0.80 - (-0.44) = +1.24\ \mathrm{V}$. The silver value is not doubled.",
             feedback=["You added the numbers instead of subtracting the negative one: 0.80 - 0.44.", "", "You doubled the silver potential: 2(0.80) + 0.44 = 2.04.", "You subtracted the wrong way round."], hint="Subtract the less positive from the more positive, taking care with the minus sign."),
        QUIZ(r"A reaction has $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = +1.10\ \mathrm{V}$ and $n = 2$. What is the sign of $\Delta G^\circ$ and the size of $K$?",
             [r"Positive $\Delta G^\circ$, $K < 1$", r"Negative $\Delta G^\circ$, $K \gg 1$", r"Negative $\Delta G^\circ$, $K < 1$", r"Zero $\Delta G^\circ$, $K = 1$"], 1,
             r"$\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ < 0$ and $\ln K = nFE^\circ/RT > 0$, so $K > 1$ (here about $10^{37}$).",
             feedback=["That would need a negative E-standard.", "", "A negative delta G always goes with K greater than 1.", "That would need E-standard equal to zero."], hint=r"Use $\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ$."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Use the table to find the cell reaction and $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ for a cell with $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}/Fe}$ ($-0.44$ V) and $\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}$ ($+0.80$ V), and calculate $\Delta G^\circ$.",
           r"Silver is reduced and iron oxidised: $\mathrm{Fe} + 2\mathrm{Ag^+} \rightarrow \mathrm{Fe^{2+}} + 2\mathrm{Ag}$. $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 0.80 - (-0.44) = +1.24\ \mathrm{V}$. $\Delta G^\circ = -2\times96\,500\times1.24 = -2.39\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = -239\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"Show that bromine can oxidise iodide ions but not chloride ions. ($E^\circ$: $\mathrm{Br_2/Br^-} = 1.07$, $\mathrm{I_2/I^-} = 0.54$, $\mathrm{Cl_2/Cl^-} = 1.36$.)",
           r"With iodide: $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.07 - 0.54 = +0.53\ \mathrm{V}$ (feasible): $\mathrm{Br_2} + 2\mathrm{I^-} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{Br^-} + \mathrm{I_2}$. With chloride: $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.07 - 1.36 = -0.29\ \mathrm{V}$ (not feasible)."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the equilibrium constant for $\mathrm{Zn} + \mathrm{Cu^{2+}} \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + \mathrm{Cu}$ at $298\ \mathrm{K}$, given $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.10\ \mathrm{V}$.",
           r"$\ln K = \frac{nFE^\circ}{RT} = \frac{2\times96\,500\times1.10}{8.31\times298} = 85.7$, so $K = e^{85.7} = 1.7\times10^{37}$. The reaction goes essentially to completion."),
        PQ(r"Dilute sulfuric acid does not react with copper, but hot concentrated sulfuric acid does. Explain the first observation using $E^\circ$ values, and state why a positive $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ does not always predict a visible reaction.",
           r"For copper with $\mathrm{H^+}$: $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 0.00 - 0.34 = -0.34\ \mathrm{V}$, so the reaction is not feasible. A positive $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ shows only that the reaction is feasible, not how fast. Conditions may not be standard, and a very high activation energy can make the rate negligible."),
        TIP("In the exam: calculating E-standard of a cell, writing the cell reaction, and linking it to delta G or K are Paper 2 items. Paper 1 asks which reaction is feasible from a data table. State R, F and the temperature you use."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ(\text{reduced}) - E^\circ(\text{oxidised})$ = more positive minus less positive. Do not multiply $E^\circ$ by coefficients.",
            "Balance the electrons in the half-equations, then add them to get the cell reaction. Check atoms and charge.",
            r"A positive $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ means the reaction is feasible: $\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ < 0$, and $K > 1$ ($\ln K = nFE^\circ/RT$).",
            r"Use $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$, $T = 298\ \mathrm{K}$.",
            "Feasible does not mean fast: kinetics and non-standard conditions can stop a predicted reaction."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 16. The Nernst equation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ELE, topic="The Nernst equation",
    title="The Nernst equation: e.m.f. under non-standard conditions",
    slug="chu1-nernst-equation",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use E = E-standard - (RT/nF) ln Q, and its 298 K form with 0.0591/n, to calculate cell and half-cell potentials at any concentration, find the e.m.f. of concentration cells, and link E to pH and Ksp.",
    objectives=[
        "State the Nernst equation and write the reaction quotient Q for a cell reaction",
        "Calculate E for a cell or half-cell at non-standard concentrations",
        "Calculate the e.m.f. of a concentration cell and of a hydrogen electrode at a given pH",
        "Explain why a cell's e.m.f. falls to zero at equilibrium",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Nernst equation", "Reaction quotient Q", "Concentration cell", "E and pH", "E at equilibrium", "0.0591 V factor"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A torch battery gives a steady light at first, then the light grows dim. The chemicals in the cell are being used up, so the concentrations are changing, and the voltage falls. Standard electrode potentials apply only when every solution is $1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. To predict the voltage at any concentration we need the **Nernst equation**."),
        H("From free energy to the Nernst equation"),
        T(r"For any concentrations, the free energy change is $\Delta G = \Delta G^\circ + RT\ln Q$, where $Q$ is the **reaction quotient**: it has the same form as the equilibrium constant expression, but uses the concentrations at that moment. Since $\Delta G = -nFE$ and $\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ$, dividing by $-nF$ gives the result."),
        D(r"For a cell reaction involving the transfer of $n$ electrons:", title="Nernst equation",
          formula=r"E = E^\circ - \frac{RT}{nF}\ln Q",
          note="Q for aA + bB -> cC + dD is [C]^c [D]^d / [A]^a [B]^b. Solids and liquid water are left out."),
        T(r"At $298\ \mathrm{K}$, with $R = 8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$ and $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, the constant $\frac{RT}{F}\ln 10 = \frac{8.31\times298}{96\,500}\times2.303 = 0.0591\ \mathrm{V}$. Converting to common logarithms gives the form you will use most:"),
        F(r"E = E^\circ - \frac{0.0591}{n}\log_{10} Q \qquad (298\ \mathrm{K})"),
        T(r"For a single half-cell written as a reduction, $\text{oxidised form} + ne^- \rightleftharpoons \text{reduced form}$, the half-cell potential is $E = E^\circ + \frac{0.0591}{n}\log_{10}\dfrac{[\text{oxidised}]}{[\text{reduced}]}$. Raising the concentration of the oxidised form makes the potential more positive, in agreement with Le Chatelier's principle."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the zinc-copper cell at non-standard concentrations", intro=r"The cell $\mathrm{Zn(s)} | \mathrm{Zn^{2+}}(1.00\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) \,||\, \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) | \mathrm{Cu(s)}$ has $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.10\ \mathrm{V}$. Calculate its e.m.f.", steps=[
            S("Cell reaction and Q (solids omitted)", math=r"\mathrm{Zn} + \mathrm{Cu^{2+}} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + \mathrm{Cu}, \qquad Q = \frac{[\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]}{[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}]} = \frac{1.00}{0.010} = 100"),
            S("Substitute with $n = 2$", math=r"E = 1.10 - \frac{0.0591}{2}\log_{10}100 = 1.10 - 0.02955\times2"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"E = 1.10 - 0.0591 = 1.04\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("Interpretation", text="Making the copper solution more dilute reduces the driving force, so the e.m.f. is lower than the standard value."),
        ], check=r"1.10 - 0.0591 = 1.0409"),
        H("Concentration cells"),
        T(r"A **concentration cell** has the same electrode material and the same ions in both half-cells, but different concentrations. $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 0$, so all of the e.m.f. comes from the Nernst term. The more concentrated side is the cathode (the ions are reduced there), and the more dilute side is the anode."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a copper concentration cell", intro=r"$\mathrm{Cu(s)} | \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(0.0010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) \,||\, \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) | \mathrm{Cu(s)}$. Calculate the e.m.f.", steps=[
            S("Cell reaction", math=r"\mathrm{Cu(s)} + \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(0.10) \rightarrow \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(0.0010) + \mathrm{Cu(s)}"),
            S("Q", math=r"Q = \frac{0.0010}{0.10} = 0.010"),
            S("Nernst equation with $E^\\circ = 0$ and $n = 2$", math=r"E = 0 - \frac{0.0591}{2}\log_{10}(0.010) = -0.02955\times(-2)"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"E = +0.0591\ \mathrm{V}"),
        ]),
        H("Linking to pH and to Ksp"),
        T(r"Hydrogen electrodes respond to pH. For $2\mathrm{H^+} + 2e^- \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{H_2}$ with $\mathrm{H_2}$ at 1 bar, $E = 0.00 + \frac{0.0591}{2}\log[\mathrm{H^+}]^2 = 0.0591\log[\mathrm{H^+}] = -0.0591\ \text{pH}$. This is how a pH meter works in principle (practical meters use a glass electrode). A silver electrode dipped in a solution with a known chloride concentration lets us find $[\mathrm{Ag^+}]$ from $K_{sp}$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: hydrogen electrode and silver electrode", intro=r"(a) A hydrogen electrode ($\mathrm{H_2}$ at 1 bar) is dipped in $0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ ethanoic acid of pH $2.87$. Find its potential. (b) A silver electrode is in a saturated solution of AgCl containing $0.0100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ $\mathrm{Cl^-}$. Find its potential, given $K_{sp}(\mathrm{AgCl}) = 1.8\times10^{-10}$ and $E^\circ(\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag}) = 0.80\ \mathrm{V}$.", steps=[
            S("(a) Use $E = -0.0591\\,\\text{pH}$", math=r"E = -0.0591\times2.87 = -0.170\ \mathrm{V}"),
            S("(b) Find $[\\mathrm{Ag^+}]$ from $K_{sp}$", math=r"[\mathrm{Ag^+}] = \frac{1.8\times10^{-10}}{0.0100} = 1.8\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}"),
            S("Half-cell Nernst equation, $n = 1$", math=r"E = 0.80 + 0.0591\log_{10}(1.8\times10^{-8}) = 0.80 + 0.0591\times(-7.745)"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"E = 0.80 - 0.458 = +0.34\ \mathrm{V}"),
        ]),
        H("At equilibrium"),
        T(r"As a cell works, reactants are used and products build up, so $Q$ rises. The Nernst equation gives a smaller and smaller $E$. When $Q = K$ the reaction is at equilibrium, and $E = 0$: the cell is flat. At that point $0 = E^\circ - \frac{0.0591}{n}\log K$, so $\log_{10}K = \frac{nE^\circ}{0.0591}$. This agrees with $\ln K = nFE^\circ/RT$ from the previous lesson."),
        MISTAKE("Putting Q upside down", r"$Q$ is products over reactants for the cell reaction **as written**. If you write it the wrong way round, the correction term has the wrong sign. A check: making the reactants more concentrated must raise $E$."),
        MISTAKE("Including solids in Q", r"Solid metals have constant activity and are omitted. $Q = [\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]/[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}]$, not including $\mathrm{Zn}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}$."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to divide by n", r"The correction is $0.0591/n$. For a two-electron reaction use $0.02955$, not $0.0591$."),
        QUIZ(r"In the cell $\mathrm{Zn} + \mathrm{Cu^{2+}} \rightarrow \mathrm{Zn^{2+}} + \mathrm{Cu}$, the concentration of $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ is decreased. What happens to the e.m.f.?",
             ["It increases", "It does not change", "It decreases", "It becomes exactly 1.10 V"], 2,
             r"$Q = [\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]/[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}]$ increases, so the term $-\frac{0.0591}{n}\log Q$ is more negative and $E$ falls. Less reactant means less driving force.",
             feedback=["Removing reactant reduces the driving force.", "E depends on concentration through Q.", "", "1.10 V applies only at standard concentrations."], hint="What happens to Q?"),
        QUIZ(r"Which expression gives $Q$ for the cell reaction $\mathrm{Mg(s)} + 2\mathrm{Ag^+(aq)} \rightarrow \mathrm{Mg^{2+}(aq)} + 2\mathrm{Ag(s)}$?",
             [r"$\dfrac{[\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]}{[\mathrm{Ag^+}]}$", r"$\dfrac{[\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]}{[\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2}$", r"$\dfrac{[\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}][\mathrm{Ag}]^2}{[\mathrm{Mg}][\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2}$", r"$[\mathrm{Ag^+}]^2[\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]$"], 1,
             r"Products over reactants, each to its coefficient, with solids omitted.",
             feedback=["The silver ion has a coefficient of 2, so it is squared.", "", "Solids are not included.", "The expression is a ratio, products divided by reactants."], hint="Leave out the solids."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Calculate the e.m.f. of the cell $\mathrm{Zn(s)} | \mathrm{Zn^{2+}}(0.10\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) \,||\, \mathrm{Cu^{2+}}(1.0\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}) | \mathrm{Cu(s)}$. ($E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.10\ \mathrm{V}$.)",
           r"$Q = \frac{[\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]}{[\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}]} = \frac{0.10}{1.0} = 0.10$. $E = 1.10 - \frac{0.0591}{2}\log(0.10) = 1.10 + 0.02955 = 1.13\ \mathrm{V}$. The e.m.f. is larger than standard, because the reactant is concentrated and the product dilute."),
        PQ(r"The potential of the $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}}$ half-cell is measured in a solution where $[\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}] = 0.10$ and $[\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}] = 0.010\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$. Calculate it. ($E^\circ = +0.77\ \mathrm{V}$.)",
           r"$E = 0.77 + 0.0591\log\frac{0.10}{0.010} = 0.77 + 0.0591 = 0.829\ \mathrm{V}$, about $0.83\ \mathrm{V}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why the e.m.f. of a working cell falls steadily with use and why it eventually becomes zero.",
           r"As the cell reacts, reactants are used up and products form, so $Q$ increases. The Nernst equation shows that $E$ decreases as $\ln Q$ increases. When $Q = K$ the system is at equilibrium, $\Delta G = 0$, and $E = 0$. No more work can be obtained from the cell."),
        PQ(r"A hydrogen electrode (1 bar $\mathrm{H_2}$) is placed in a solution and connected to the SHE. The e.m.f. is $0.177\ \mathrm{V}$. Calculate the pH of the solution. (Use $0.0591$ V; the hydrogen electrode in the solution is the negative terminal.)",
           r"$E = -0.0591\,\text{pH}$, so $\text{pH} = \frac{0.177}{0.0591} = 3.0$."),
        TIP("In the exam: Nernst calculations for cells and half-cells, concentration cells and links to pH or Ksp are Paper 2 items. You will be given the equation, or the 298 K form, with R and F. Show Q written out and say which concentration is which."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$E = E^\circ - \frac{RT}{nF}\ln Q$; at 298 K, $E = E^\circ - \frac{0.0591}{n}\log_{10}Q$ (with $R = 8.31$, $F = 96\,500$).",
            r"$Q$ = products over reactants for the cell reaction as written; solids are omitted.",
            r"Half-cell: $E = E^\circ + \frac{0.0591}{n}\log\frac{[\text{ox}]}{[\text{red}]}$. Hydrogen electrode: $E = -0.0591\,\text{pH}$.",
            "A concentration cell has E-standard = 0; the more concentrated side is the cathode.",
            r"At equilibrium $Q = K$ and $E = 0$, so $\log K = nE^\circ / 0.0591$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 17. Fuel cells
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ELE, topic="Fuel cells",
    title="Hydrogen and other fuel cells",
    slug="chu1-fuel-cells",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Describe how a hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell works in acidic and alkaline electrolytes, write the electrode equations, calculate E-standard, free energy and theoretical efficiency, and weigh up the advantages and limits.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the construction and operation of a hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell",
        "Write electrode half-equations for acidic and alkaline electrolytes and calculate E-standard",
        "Calculate the free energy change, the theoretical efficiency and the amount of hydrogen used for a given current",
        "Compare fuel cells with batteries and combustion engines, including limitations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Fuel cell", "Anode and cathode reactions", "Acidic and alkaline electrolyte", "Theoretical efficiency", "Hydrogen consumption", "Advantages and limitations"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In a petrol generator the chemical energy of the fuel is turned into heat, then into motion, then into electricity. Much of the energy is lost as heat at each stage. A **fuel cell** skips most of these stages. It turns the chemical energy of a fuel straight into electrical energy, in an electrochemical cell that is supplied continuously with fuel and oxygen."),
        D(r"A **fuel cell** is an electrochemical cell in which a fuel (for example hydrogen) and an oxidant (oxygen from the air) are fed continuously to the electrodes. It produces electricity for as long as they are supplied, because the electrodes and electrolyte are not used up.", title="Fuel cell"),
        T(r"Unlike an ordinary battery, the reactants are not stored inside. Each electrode is porous and carries a platinum-based catalyst, so that the gas can reach the electrolyte. The two electrodes are separated by an electrolyte that lets ions pass but not electrons or gases."),
        H("The hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell with an acidic electrolyte"),
        T(r"In the acid-electrolyte cell (for example a proton exchange membrane), hydrogen is fed to the **anode**, where it is oxidised, and oxygen to the **cathode**, where it is reduced. Electrons flow through the external circuit, and $\mathrm{H^+}$ ions move through the electrolyte."),
        CODE("""Anode (oxidation):      2H2 -> 4H+ + 4e-
Cathode (reduction):    O2 + 4H+ + 4e- -> 2H2O
Overall:                2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O

E-standard values:      O2 + 4H+ + 4e- <=> 2H2O     +1.23 V
                        2H+ + 2e- <=> H2             0.00 V
E-standard (cell) = 1.23 - 0.00 = +1.23 V""", "text", title="Acidic electrolyte"),
        T(r"In the alkaline cell (for example potassium hydroxide solution), the hydroxide ion carries the charge through the electrolyte:"),
        CODE("""Anode (oxidation):      2H2 + 4OH- -> 4H2O + 4e-
Cathode (reduction):    O2 + 2H2O + 4e- -> 4OH-
Overall:                2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O

E-standard values:      O2 + 2H2O + 4e- <=> 4OH-     +0.40 V
                        2H2O + 2e- <=> H2 + 2OH-      -0.83 V
E-standard (cell) = 0.40 - (-0.83) = +1.23 V""", "text", title="Alkaline electrolyte"),
        T(r"Both give the same overall reaction and the same $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$, because they describe the same overall change. This is a useful check on your half-equations: they must add up to the overall equation, with the same electrons on each side and ions cancelling."),
        EX("Worked example 1: combining half-equations", intro=r"Show that the alkaline half-equations add up to $2\mathrm{H_2} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2O}$.", steps=[
            S("Write both", math=r"2\mathrm{H_2} + 4\mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow 4\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^-, \qquad \mathrm{O_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^- \rightarrow 4\mathrm{OH^-}"),
            S("Add (electrons cancel: 4 each)", math=r"2\mathrm{H_2} + 4\mathrm{OH^-} + \mathrm{O_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O} \rightarrow 4\mathrm{H_2O} + 4\mathrm{OH^-}"),
            S("Cancel $4\\mathrm{OH^-}$ and $2\\mathrm{H_2O}$", math=r"2\mathrm{H_2} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2O}"),
        ]),
        H("Energy and efficiency"),
        T(r"In a heat engine, the best efficiency depends on the temperatures, and is usually well below 50%. In a fuel cell, the maximum electrical work obtainable is $-\Delta G$. The **theoretical efficiency** is $\Delta G/\Delta H$. For hydrogen forming liquid water, $\Delta H = -286\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ of $\mathrm{H_2}$ (data value)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: free energy and theoretical efficiency", intro=r"Calculate (a) $\Delta G^\circ$ per mole of $\mathrm{H_2}$ for $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.23\ \mathrm{V}$, and (b) the theoretical efficiency. ($F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$)", steps=[
            S("(a) For 1 mol of $\\mathrm{H_2}$, $n = 2$", math=r"\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ = -2\times96\,500\times1.23 = -2.374\times10^{5}\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}} = -237\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}"),
            S("(b) Efficiency", math=r"\frac{\Delta G}{\Delta H} = \frac{-237}{-286} = 0.83 = 83\%"),
            S("Comment", text="The real efficiency is lower: the working voltage is only about 0.6 to 0.8 V, because of resistance and slow electrode reactions. It is still higher than for most heat engines."),
        ]),
        T(r"Every mole of $\mathrm{H_2}$ gives $2$ moles of electrons, so $2F = 193\,000\ \mathrm{C}$ of charge. The charge passed is $Q = It$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: hydrogen consumption", intro=r"A hydrogen fuel cell delivers a steady current of $2.0\ \mathrm{A}$ for $1.0$ hour. What volume of hydrogen at room temperature and pressure (rtp, molar volume $24\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}$) is used? ($F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$)", steps=[
            S("Charge", math=r"Q = It = 2.0\times3600 = 7200\ \mathrm{C}"),
            S("Moles of electrons", math=r"\frac{7200}{96\,500} = 0.0746\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Each $\\mathrm{H_2}$ releases 2 electrons", math=r"n(\mathrm{H_2}) = \frac{0.0746}{2} = 0.0373\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Volume at rtp", math=r"V = 0.0373\times24 = 0.895\ \mathrm{dm^3}"),
        ]),
        T(r"Other fuels can be used. In a **methanol fuel cell** the anode reaction is $\mathrm{CH_3OH} + \mathrm{H_2O} \rightarrow \mathrm{CO_2} + 6\mathrm{H^+} + 6e^-$ and the cathode reaction is $\tfrac32\mathrm{O_2} + 6\mathrm{H^+} + 6e^- \rightarrow 3\mathrm{H_2O}$, so the overall reaction is $\mathrm{CH_3OH} + \tfrac32\mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{CO_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O}$. Methanol is a liquid, so it is easier to store than hydrogen, but the cell produces carbon dioxide."),
        KEY("Weighing up fuel cells", "Give a balanced answer in the exam.", cards=[
            ["Advantages", r"Only water is formed at the point of use (for hydrogen). Higher efficiency than a heat engine. Quiet, few moving parts, works as long as fuel is supplied, and can be used where the electricity grid is weak, for example in remote areas, as a back-up supply."],
            ["Limitations", r"Hydrogen is difficult and costly to store and transport (a gas at high pressure, or very cold liquid). Most hydrogen today is made from fossil fuels, which produces carbon dioxide; hydrogen from electrolysis is clean only if the electricity is. Platinum catalysts are expensive. The refuelling network is small."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying a fuel cell is a source of energy", r"A fuel cell is a converter. The energy comes from the fuel. If the hydrogen is made using fossil fuels, the fuel cell is not carbon-free overall."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up anode and cathode", r"Oxidation at the anode: hydrogen loses electrons. Reduction at the cathode: oxygen gains them. This is true for every cell. The anode is the negative terminal in a fuel cell, because it releases electrons."),
        QUIZ(r"In a hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell with an acidic electrolyte, which reaction occurs at the anode?",
             [r"$\mathrm{O_2} + 4\mathrm{H^+} + 4e^- \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2O}$", r"$\mathrm{H_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2O}$", r"$\mathrm{H_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H^+} + 2e^-$", r"$\mathrm{O_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^- \rightarrow 4\mathrm{OH^-}$"], 2,
             r"The anode is where oxidation happens. Hydrogen loses electrons: $\mathrm{H_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H^+} + 2e^-$.",
             feedback=["This is the cathode reaction (reduction of oxygen).", "This is not a balanced half-equation (no electrons shown).", "", "This is the cathode reaction in an alkaline cell."], hint="The anode is where oxidation occurs."),
        QUIZ(r"The theoretical efficiency of a hydrogen fuel cell is about 83%. Which statement is correct?",
             [r"It is the ratio $\Delta G / \Delta H$", r"It is always achieved in practice", r"It means 17% of the hydrogen is not burned", r"It is calculated using the molar mass of hydrogen"], 0,
             r"The maximum electrical work is $-\Delta G$, and the energy available from the reaction is $-\Delta H$, so the efficiency is $\Delta G/\Delta H$.",
             feedback=["", "Resistance and slow electrode reactions lower the real efficiency.", "Efficiency refers to energy, not to the amount of fuel.", "The molar mass does not enter the calculation."], hint="Which energy quantities are compared?"),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Write the half-equations at each electrode of a hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell with an alkaline electrolyte, and calculate $E^\circ_{\text{cell}}$ from $E^\circ(\mathrm{O_2/OH^-}) = +0.40\ \mathrm{V}$ and $E^\circ(\mathrm{H_2O/H_2,\,OH^-}) = -0.83\ \mathrm{V}$.",
           r"Anode: $2\mathrm{H_2} + 4\mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow 4\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^-$. Cathode: $\mathrm{O_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^- \rightarrow 4\mathrm{OH^-}$. $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 0.40 - (-0.83) = +1.23\ \mathrm{V}$."),
        PQ(r"A fuel cell supplies $5.0\ \mathrm{A}$ continuously. Calculate the time needed to use $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of hydrogen at rtp ($24\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}$; $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$).",
           r"$n(\mathrm{H_2}) = 1.00/24 = 0.04167\ \mathrm{mol}$. Charge $= 0.04167\times2\times96\,500 = 8042\ \mathrm{C}$. $t = \frac{8042}{5.0} = 1608\ \mathrm{s} = 26.8\ \mathrm{min}$."),
        PQ(r"Five identical hydrogen-oxygen cells, each giving $0.70\ \mathrm{V}$ under load, are connected in series to make a stack. Calculate the voltage of the stack, and the power when it supplies $2.0\ \mathrm{A}$.",
           r"Voltage $= 5\times0.70 = 3.5\ \mathrm{V}$. Power $= IV = 2.0\times3.5 = 7.0\ \mathrm{W}$."),
        PQ(r"Discuss one advantage and two limitations of using hydrogen fuel cells to supply electricity to a rural health centre with an unreliable grid supply.",
           r"Advantage: electricity is made quietly with only water as a product at the point of use, and the cell works for as long as hydrogen is supplied, so it can serve as a back-up. Limitations: hydrogen must be produced, stored and transported, which is costly and needs safe handling (it is flammable). The cells use expensive catalysts, and if the hydrogen is made from fossil fuels there is still carbon dioxide emitted elsewhere."),
        TIP("In the exam: fuel cells are tested in Paper 2 (half-equations, E-standard, delta G, calculations from current, advantages and disadvantages) and in Paper 1 (anode reaction, overall equation). Use the electrolyte stated in the question."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A fuel cell converts the chemical energy of a continuously supplied fuel directly into electrical energy.",
            r"Acid: anode $2\mathrm{H_2} \rightarrow 4\mathrm{H^+} + 4e^-$; cathode $\mathrm{O_2} + 4\mathrm{H^+} + 4e^- \rightarrow 2\mathrm{H_2O}$. Alkali: anode $2\mathrm{H_2} + 4\mathrm{OH^-} \rightarrow 4\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^-$; cathode $\mathrm{O_2} + 2\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^- \rightarrow 4\mathrm{OH^-}$.",
            r"Both give $E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = 1.23\ \mathrm{V}$. $\Delta G^\circ = -237\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ of $\mathrm{H_2}$; theoretical efficiency $= \Delta G/\Delta H = 83\%$.",
            r"Hydrogen used: $n(\mathrm{H_2}) = It/(2F)$.",
            "Advantages: efficient and clean at the point of use. Limits: hydrogen production and storage, cost of catalysts, working voltage below E-standard."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 18. Quantitative electrolysis
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=ELE, topic="Quantitative electrolysis",
    title="Quantitative electrolysis and Faraday's laws",
    slug="chu1-quantitative-electrolysis",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Predict the products of electrolysis, and use Q = It, the Faraday constant and half-equations to calculate masses, gas volumes, times and currents, including cells in series and the extraction of aluminium.",
    objectives=[
        "Predict the products at each electrode in an electrolysis using E-standard, concentration and the type of electrode",
        "Calculate the charge from current and time, and the moles of electrons using F = 96 500 C per mole",
        "Calculate the mass or gas volume formed, or the time or current needed",
        "Apply the method to electroplating, copper refining, cells in series, and the extraction of aluminium",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Electrolysis", "Selective discharge", "Q = It", "Faraday constant", "Moles of electrons", "Cells in series", "Extraction of aluminium"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A jeweller in Foumban plates a thin layer of silver on a brass ornament. A refinery purifies copper from ore. A factory makes chlorine and sodium hydroxide from brine. All three use **electrolysis**: an electric current that forces a non-spontaneous chemical change. In industry the question is always quantitative: how much current, for how long, to make a certain mass?"),
        D(r"**Electrolysis** is the decomposition of an ionic compound, molten or in solution, by passing an electric current through it using two electrodes. At the **cathode** (negative) reduction occurs; at the **anode** (positive) oxidation occurs.", title="Electrolysis"),
        T(r"Compare this with a cell that gives electricity. In an electrolytic cell the battery pushes electrons: the cathode is negative, the anode is positive. In both kinds of cell, oxidation is at the anode and reduction at the cathode."),
        H("Which ion is discharged? Selective discharge"),
        T(r"In aqueous solution there are ions from the compound **and** ions from water ($\mathrm{H^+}$ and $\mathrm{OH^-}$). Three things decide which is discharged:"),
        KEY("Selective discharge", "Use all three factors.", cards=[
            ["E-standard", r"At the cathode the species with the **more positive** $E^\circ$ is reduced first (for example $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ before $\mathrm{H^+}$). At the anode the species that is **most easily oxidised** goes first."],
            ["Concentration", r"A concentrated solution of $\mathrm{Cl^-}$ gives chlorine at the anode, even though $E^\circ$ for $\mathrm{O_2/H_2O}$ would suggest oxygen. In dilute chloride solution, oxygen is formed instead."],
            ["Electrode material", r"Inert electrodes (platinum, graphite) take no part. An active electrode such as copper can itself be oxidised."],
        ]),
        CODE("""Electrolyte (aqueous)       Electrodes   Cathode product   Anode product
Copper(II) sulfate          graphite     Cu                O2 (4OH- -> O2 + 2H2O + 4e-)
Copper(II) sulfate          copper       Cu                Cu dissolves (Cu -> Cu2+ + 2e-)
Concentrated sodium chloride graphite    H2                Cl2
Dilute sodium chloride      graphite     H2                O2
Molten aluminium oxide      graphite     Al                O2 (reacts with the carbon anodes)""", "text", title="Products of electrolysis"),
        H("The quantitative link: Q = It and the Faraday constant"),
        D(r"The charge $Q$ passed (in coulombs) is the current $I$ (amperes) times the time $t$ (seconds). One mole of electrons carries a charge of $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C}$ (the **Faraday constant**; check your textbook for $96\,485$).", title="Charge and moles of electrons",
          formula=r"Q = It, \qquad n(e^-) = \frac{Q}{F} = \frac{It}{96\,500}",
          note="Convert minutes or hours into seconds before multiplying. Then use the half-equation to link moles of electrons to moles of product."),
        KEY("The four-step method", "The same every time.", cards=[
            ["1", "Calculate the charge Q = It."],
            ["2", "Divide by F to get moles of electrons."],
            ["3", r"Use the half-equation (for example $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{Cu}$) to get moles of product."],
            ["4", r"Convert to mass ($n \times A_r$ or $M_r$) or to volume ($n \times 24\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ at rtp)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: electrolysis of copper(II) sulfate with graphite electrodes", intro=r"A current of $2.00\ \mathrm{A}$ is passed through aqueous copper(II) sulfate for $30.0$ minutes. Calculate (a) the mass of copper deposited ($A_r = 63.5$) and (b) the volume of oxygen at the anode at rtp ($24\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}$). ($F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$)", steps=[
            S("Charge", math=r"Q = It = 2.00\times(30.0\times60) = 3600\ \mathrm{C}"),
            S("Moles of electrons", math=r"n(e^-) = \frac{3600}{96\,500} = 0.03731\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("(a) $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}$, so 2 mol of $e^-$ per mole of Cu", math=r"n(\mathrm{Cu}) = \frac{0.03731}{2} = 0.01865\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Mass", math=r"m = 0.01865\times63.5 = 1.18\ \mathrm{g}"),
            S("(b) $4\\mathrm{OH^-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{O_2} + 2\\mathrm{H_2O} + 4e^-$, so 4 mol of $e^-$ per mole of $\\mathrm{O_2}$", math=r"n(\mathrm{O_2}) = \frac{0.03731}{4} = 0.009327\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Volume", math=r"V = 0.009327\times24 = 0.224\ \mathrm{dm^3}"),
        ], check=r"\text{mass ratio: } 0.01865\ \mathrm{mol\ Cu}\ \text{and}\ 0.009327\ \mathrm{mol\ O_2} = 2 : 1"),
        EX("Worked example 2: electroplating silver", intro=r"How long (in minutes) does it take to plate $0.500\ \mathrm{g}$ of silver ($A_r = 108$) onto a spoon, using a steady current of $1.50\ \mathrm{A}$ and silver nitrate solution?", steps=[
            S("Moles of silver", math=r"n(\mathrm{Ag}) = \frac{0.500}{108} = 4.630\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("$\\mathrm{Ag^+} + e^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}$: 1 mol of $e^-$ per mole of Ag. Charge needed", math=r"Q = n(e^-)\times F = 4.630\times10^{-3}\times96\,500 = 446.8\ \mathrm{C}"),
            S("Time", math=r"t = \frac{Q}{I} = \frac{446.8}{1.50} = 297.8\ \mathrm{s} = 4.96\ \mathrm{min}"),
        ]),
        T(r"When two electrolytic cells are connected in series, the **same current** flows for the **same time** in both, so the same charge passes through each. The masses deposited are linked through the moles of electrons."),
        EX("Worked example 3: copper and silver cells in series", intro=r"A copper(II) sulfate cell and a silver nitrate cell are connected in series. $0.635\ \mathrm{g}$ of copper is deposited in the first cell. What mass of silver ($A_r = 108$) is deposited in the second?", steps=[
            S("Moles of copper", math=r"n(\mathrm{Cu}) = \frac{0.635}{63.5} = 0.0100\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Moles of electrons (2 per Cu)", math=r"n(e^-) = 0.0200\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("The same moles of electrons deposit silver (1 per Ag)", math=r"n(\mathrm{Ag}) = 0.0200\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Mass of silver", math=r"m = 0.0200\times108 = 2.16\ \mathrm{g}"),
        ]),
        H("Industrial case: aluminium"),
        T(r"Aluminium is too reactive to be obtained by reducing its oxide with carbon, so aluminium oxide is dissolved in molten cryolite ($\mathrm{Na_3AlF_6}$), which lowers the working temperature from over $2000\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to about $950\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$, and is electrolysed with carbon electrodes. Cathode: $\mathrm{Al^{3+}} + 3e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{Al}$. Anode: $2\mathrm{O^{2-}} \rightarrow \mathrm{O_2} + 4e^-$, and the oxygen burns away the carbon anodes, which must be replaced. Three moles of electrons are needed per mole of aluminium, which is why the process uses huge amounts of electricity."),
        EX("Worked example 4: charge to make aluminium", intro=r"Calculate the charge needed to produce $1.00\ \mathrm{kg}$ of aluminium ($A_r = 27$), and the time if a cell passes $50.0\ \mathrm{kA}$ (assume 100% efficiency).", steps=[
            S("Moles of aluminium", math=r"n(\mathrm{Al}) = \frac{1000}{27} = 37.04\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Moles of electrons (3 per Al)", math=r"n(e^-) = 3\times37.04 = 111.1\ \mathrm{mol}"),
            S("Charge", math=r"Q = 111.1\times96\,500 = 1.07\times10^{7}\ \mathrm{C}"),
            S("Time at $5.00\\times10^4\\ \\mathrm{A}$", math=r"t = \frac{1.07\times10^{7}}{5.00\times10^{4}} = 214\ \mathrm{s}"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong number of electrons", r"Look at the half-equation. $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ needs 2, $\mathrm{Ag^+}$ needs 1, $\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$ needs 3, and $\mathrm{O_2}$ from hydroxide releases 4."),
        MISTAKE("Saying hydrogen is always formed at the cathode", r"Hydrogen forms only if $\mathrm{H^+}$ (or water) is discharged in preference to the metal ion. From copper(II) sulfate solution, copper is deposited, because $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ has a more positive $E^\circ$ than $\mathrm{H^+}$."),
        QUIZ(r"What mass of silver ($A_r = 108$) is deposited by a current of $2.00\ \mathrm{A}$ in $965\ \mathrm{s}$? ($F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$)",
             [r"$1.08\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$2.16\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$4.32\ \mathrm{g}$", r"$0.54\ \mathrm{g}$"], 1,
             r"$Q = 2.00\times965 = 1930\ \mathrm{C}$, so $n(e^-) = 0.0200\ \mathrm{mol}$. One electron per Ag gives $0.0200\times108 = 2.16\ \mathrm{g}$.",
             feedback=["You used 0.0100 mol: this is the mass if the half-equation needed two electrons.", "", "You multiplied by 2 instead of using one electron per silver ion.", "This is a quarter of the correct mass."], hint=r"Find $Q$, then $n(e^-)$, then use $\mathrm{Ag^+} + e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{Ag}$."),
        QUIZ(r"Concentrated aqueous sodium chloride is electrolysed with graphite electrodes. What are the products at the cathode and anode?",
             [r"Sodium and chlorine", r"Hydrogen and chlorine", r"Hydrogen and oxygen", r"Sodium and oxygen"], 1,
             r"Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so $\mathrm{H^+}$ is reduced at the cathode ($2\mathrm{H^+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2}$). At high concentration chloride is oxidised: $2\mathrm{Cl^-} \rightarrow \mathrm{Cl_2} + 2e^-$.",
             feedback=["Sodium is not formed from aqueous solution.", "", "Oxygen would form at the anode only in dilute chloride solutions.", "Neither sodium nor oxygen is the product here."], hint="Use selective discharge: concentration decides at the anode."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A current of $1.20\ \mathrm{A}$ is passed for $45.0$ minutes through copper(II) sulfate solution using copper electrodes. Calculate the change in mass of the anode. ($A_r(\mathrm{Cu}) = 63.5$; $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$)",
           r"$Q = 1.20\times2700 = 3240\ \mathrm{C}$. $n(e^-) = 3240/96\,500 = 0.03358\ \mathrm{mol}$. The anode reaction is $\mathrm{Cu} \rightarrow \mathrm{Cu^{2+}} + 2e^-$, so $n(\mathrm{Cu}) = 0.01679\ \mathrm{mol}$. The anode loses $0.01679\times63.5 = 1.07\ \mathrm{g}$ (equal to the mass gained by the cathode: this is how copper is refined)."),
        PQ(r"In an experiment, $0.318\ \mathrm{g}$ of copper is deposited by a current of $1.00\ \mathrm{A}$ in $965\ \mathrm{s}$. Calculate the experimental value of the Faraday constant ($A_r = 63.5$), and use $e = 1.60\times10^{-19}\ \mathrm{C}$ to calculate the Avogadro constant.",
           r"$n(\mathrm{Cu}) = 0.318/63.5 = 5.008\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol}$, so $n(e^-) = 1.002\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol}$. $Q = 965\ \mathrm{C}$. $F = Q/n(e^-) = 965/(1.002\times10^{-2}) = 9.63\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$. $L = F/e = \frac{96\,500}{1.60\times10^{-19}} = 6.03\times10^{23}\ \mathrm{mol^{-1}}$."),
        PQ(r"Dilute sulfuric acid is electrolysed using platinum electrodes with a current of $5.00\ \mathrm{A}$. Calculate the time needed to collect $1.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of hydrogen at rtp ($24\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}$; $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$).",
           r"$n(\mathrm{H_2}) = 1.00/24 = 0.04167\ \mathrm{mol}$. $2\mathrm{H^+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2}$, so $n(e^-) = 0.08333\ \mathrm{mol}$. $Q = 0.08333\times96\,500 = 8042\ \mathrm{C}$. $t = 8042/5.00 = 1608\ \mathrm{s} = 26.8\ \mathrm{min}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why cryolite is added in the extraction of aluminium, and why the carbon anodes must be replaced regularly.",
           r"Aluminium oxide has a very high melting point (above $2000\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$). It dissolves in molten cryolite, which lowers the temperature needed to about $950\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$, saving energy, and the dissolved oxide ions are then free to move. The oxygen formed at the anode reacts with the hot carbon to give carbon dioxide, so the anodes are gradually burned away."),
        TIP("In the exam: quantitative electrolysis (Q = It, moles of electrons, mass or volume, cells in series, extraction of aluminium, selective discharge) is tested in Paper 2 and as Paper 1 calculations. Always state the half-equation and the value of F. The practical Paper 3 may involve electrolysis with copper electrodes."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Electrolysis: reduction at the cathode (negative), oxidation at the anode (positive). Selective discharge depends on $E^\circ$, concentration and electrode material.",
            r"$Q = It$ (time in seconds) and $n(e^-) = Q/F$ with $F = 96\,500\ \mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$.",
            r"Use the half-equation to turn moles of electrons into moles of product: Cu 2, Ag 1, Al 3, $\mathrm{O_2}$ 4, $\mathrm{H_2}$ 2, $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ 2.",
            "Cells in series pass the same charge: link the products through moles of electrons.",
            "Aluminium oxide is dissolved in cryolite and electrolysed; carbon anodes burn away. Copper is refined using impure copper as anode."),
    ],
))
